window.AVICENNIAN_CORPUS={"version":"2026-10-31-argument-context-audit","title":"Late Avicennism · critical sourcebook","source":"gnamma-research/booksite","readerStatus":"Research-grade working edition with explicit argument context and no automatic keyword-extracted passages; incomplete source families visibly identified","records":[{"id":"av-vi1","file":"PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_E_AVICENNA_VI1.md","author":"Ibn Sīnā","work":"al-Shifāʾ · Ilāhiyyāt","title":"The four causes and continuing dependence","locus":"VI.1 · pp. 257–263","lang":"ar","family":"Classical Avicennism","kind":"primary","coverage":"bounded chapter · working English","topics":["causation","existence","conservation","matter","form"],"source":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=257&type=c_fbook","md":"# Primary translation fascicle E — Ibn Sīnā, *al-Shifāʾ: al-Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1\n\n**GNAMMA working English translation · 31 October 2026**\n\n**Bounded translation:** entire authorial prose of Book VI, chapter 1, **“On the kinds of causes and their conditions,”** beginning at the heading printed before p.257 and ending on printed p.263, immediately before VI.2 begins. Numbered paragraphs and intermediate headings below are editorial; the Arabic edition's original paragraphing and exact punctuation are not asserted. This is a *continuous authorial-text translation* of one chapter, not a full manuscript-critical edition.\n\n**Witness:** Ibn Sīnā, *al-Shifāʾ: al-Ilāhiyyāt*, ed. Georges Anawati and Saʿīd Zāyid, digital edited Arabic witness [beginning VI.1 (pp.257–260)](https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=257&type=c_fbook) and [continuation to the VI.2 heading (pp.260–263)](https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=264&type=c_fbook). The two links overlap at p.260; both source pages reproduce the printed variant apparatus.\n\n## I. Division of causes — printed pp.257–258\n\n**§1 [257].** We have discussed substances and accidents, their priority and posteriority, and how definitions correspond to the universal and particular things defined. It is therefore appropriate to discuss cause and effect, since these, too, are concomitants of being insofar as it is being. As you have heard, the causes are **form, matter, agent and end**.\n\n**§2 [257].** By the *formal* cause we mean that cause which is part of the constitution of a thing, and by which the thing is what it is in actuality. By the *material* cause we mean that cause which is part of a thing's constitution, through which it is what it is in potentiality, and in which resides the capacity for its existence.\n\n**§3 [257].** By the *agent* we mean the cause which gives an existence distinct from its own essence. Its own essence, in the primary intention, is not the subject in which the recipient acquires the existence of the thing whose form is realised there, such that the agent would possess, except accidentally, the potentiality for the recipient's existence. Moreover that existence must not be for the sake of the agent **insofar as it is an agent**; if it is for its sake, that must be considered under another aspect. For the metaphysicians do not mean by “agent” merely a principle of motion, as natural philosophers do; they mean a principle and giver of existence, as the Creator is to the world. A natural efficient cause, by contrast, gives no existence other than motion in one of its modes: the cause that gives existence in natural philosophy is a principle of motion. By the *end* we mean that for the sake of which something distinct from the end itself comes into existence.\n\n**§4 [258].** It appears that no cause falls outside these kinds. For a cause of something either enters into its constitution and is part of its existence, or it does not. If it enters into its constitution, it is either a part whose existence by itself is not enough to make the thing actual but constitutes its being in potentiality—called *prime matter*—or a part whose existence is the thing's becoming actual, namely form. If it is not part of the thing's existence, then it is either that for the sake of which the thing exists, or it is not. If it is the former it is the end. If not, either the thing's existence derives from it without being in it save accidentally, and it is the agent, or the thing derives its existence from it by *being in it*, and it is also its matter or subject.\n\n**§5 [258].** Thus one can count five principles under one aspect and four under another. If one distinguishes the receptive substrate which is not a constituent of a thing from matter which *is* a constituent, the principles are five. If one considers the two together because both share the character of potentiality and preparedness, they are four. One must not take matter in the sense of a receptive constituent as a principle of form *as though it were first a self-subsisting thing*: it is a principle of the composite. The receiver, as such, is a principle only accidentally, since its actuality is constituted first by form and, taken in itself, it is only potential. What is potential, precisely insofar as potential, is not itself an actual principle.\n\n**§6 [258].** It is, rather, an accidental principle [of the relevant outcome], because an accident requires the subject to be actually established before the subject becomes a cause of the accident's subsistence, whether that accident is inseparable, so that the subject is prior by essence, or separable, so that it is prior both by essence and in time. These are the kinds of causes. Moreover, when a subject constitutes and is the cause of an accident, it does not do so in precisely the same way that a matter is the cause of the composite. That is another species of cause.\n\n## II. How form is cause of matter and of the composite — printed pp.259–260\n\n**§7 [259].** When form is a cause constituting matter, it does not act in the same respect in which form causes the composite, although both cases agree that the cause is not essentially separate from what it causes.\n\n**§8 [259].** They agree in this regard, but in one case the cause does not by itself give the other its existence; rather, something else gives the existence, but does so *through* it. In the other case the cause is the proximate principle through which the effect is given its actuality, yet not by itself: a partner and another cause bring that very cause—namely the form—into existence, and it constitutes the other [matter] through itself. It thereby acts as an intermediary with a partner in giving the other actuality. Form would be like an efficient principle of matter if matter's actuality proceeded from form alone; instead form resembles *part of an efficient cause*, like one of two people moving a ship, as will become clearer later.\n\n**§9 [259].** Form is the *formal cause of the composite* that consists of form and matter. It is the form *of matter* but it is **not the formal cause of matter as such**.\n\n**§10 [259].** The efficient agent gives another thing an existence that the other thing does not have from its own essence. That existence issues from the agent without the agent's essence being a receiver of the form of the effect or joined to it as an intrinsic constituent. Rather, each essence is external to the other; neither contains a potentiality to receive the other.\n\n**§11 [259].** This does not rule out the agent's bringing about its effect in the very place where the agent itself is, in contact with its own essence. For a nature in wood can be an efficient principle of motion; motion comes to be in the matter in which that nature is, and where the nature itself is. Yet their conjunction is not such that either is a constituent part or a matter of the other. The two realities are distinct, even though they share one subject. Some agents may at a given time not be acting, and their effects may not exist; later certain conditions arise through which the agents come to be actual agents.\n\n## III. What an agent actually gives: existence, not temporal “afterness” — printed pp.260–261\n\n**§12 [260].** We have discussed this earlier. At that point the agent becomes actual, and from it a thing exists after previously not existing. That thing thus has existence, and it also has the feature of *not having existed*. Its not having existed does not come from the agent, nor does its *having come to exist after not existing*. What it receives from the agent is **its existence**. If nonexistence belonged to it in itself, then when it acquires existence its existence follows upon nonexistence and it comes to be after not having been.\n\n**§13 [260].** Accordingly, what comes from the agent in itself is existence: the effect's existence comes from another thing being in such a complete condition that the other has existence from the agent's very existence. That the effect previously did not exist is not the doing of an actual causal agent. Nonexistence can sometimes be ascribed to a cause, namely the absence of the effect's cause. But that its existence *follows* nonexistence is not something that acquires its actuality from a cause; in the stipulated case its existence cannot occur except after nonexistence. What cannot be otherwise in that respect has no separate cause for that respect. Its very existence may or may not occur, and therefore has a cause; its nonexistence may or may not occur, and therefore may have a cause. But its existence's *being after* its prior nonexistence does not have a further efficient cause as such.\n\n**§14 [260].** Someone may object that existence after nonexistence can likewise occur or not occur. We reply: if by this you mean its *existence insofar as it is existence*, nonexistence does not enter into its account. The existence itself is not necessary—that is, it is possible. Its nonnecessity is not a consequence of being after nonexistence; the nonnecessary thing is this particular occurrence of existence, which as it happens had previously been nonexistent.\n\n**§15 [260–261].** If, however, one takes its existence specifically *as existence after nonexistence*, one has to attend to its being after nonexistence and not to the bare fact that it is now existing, which happens to be preceded by nonexistence. The temporal ordering as such does not have a separate cause. This is not to say that the *existence* that follows nonexistence has no efficient cause; it may indeed be caused. What is not caused as an additional item is the abstract property that the existence is “after” the stipulated prior nonexistence.\n\n## IV. Why the effect continues to need its cause — printed pp.261–263\n\n**§16 [261].** Some suppose that a thing needs an agent and a cause only so that it should come to exist after it had not existed. Once the thing exists, the cause could disappear and the thing would remain self-sufficient. They therefore suppose that a thing needs its cause only in its *origination*; after it has come to exist it no longer needs the cause. Causes, on this view, are causes of origination alone, earlier than their effects rather than contemporaneous. **This is an erroneous supposition.**\n\n**§17 [261].** Existence after origination must either be necessary or nonnecessary. If necessary, its necessity belongs either to the essence in itself—in which case the essence requires existence and could never have originated—or to a condition. That condition is either the prior origination itself, an attribute of the essence, or something distinct from it. Its necessity cannot come from origination itself: origination has no necessity through itself, so how could it necessitate the existence of another? Moreover, origination is already past; how could it remain the cause of the necessity of existence when it is no longer present? If one says that the cause is not origination but the thing's *having undergone* origination, this would be an attribute of the originated thing and falls under the second possibility.\n\n**§18 [261–262].** Such attributes either belong to the essence insofar as it is an essence, irrespective of whether it has come to exist—in which case the necessity they produce would belong to the essence itself, and it would be intrinsically necessary—or they arise together with existence. But then we must ask whether *their own* existence is necessary. The same alternatives recur. Either infinitely many such attributes, each possible in itself, are posited, or the series ends with an attribute whose existence is necessary through something external. In the first case, all the attributes are merely possible in themselves; it has already been established that what is possible in itself is made to exist through another. The series therefore does not remove external dependence. In the second, the conclusion is explicit: originated existence remains in being through an external cause.\n\n**§19 [262].** Besides, you already know that origination means nothing but existence after not having existed. There are two items for consideration: existence, and having come after not having existed. The originating agent neither produces nor explains the fact that the thing previously did not exist. Its efficacy lies in the thing's receiving existence from it. It merely happens that, at that time, this existence follows a former nonexistence. A feature which attaches incidentally does not enter into the constitution of the thing. The preceding nonexistence therefore has no part in explaining why the originated existence requires a cause. The existence proper to that kind of essence requires a cause *even if it continues and persists*.\n\n**§20 [262].** Thus one cannot properly say that something causes the thing's existence *to have the property that it comes after not having existed*. This is not something that can be produced. Some existents necessarily cannot be after nonexistence; others necessarily are after nonexistence. But existence considered as the existence of a given essence can proceed from a cause, whereas the circumstance that it is after prior nonexistence does not in that way proceed from a cause. The thing, insofar as its existence is originated—that is, insofar as its existence is described as “after nonexistence”—has no cause *for that description itself*, whereas it has a cause insofar as existence belongs to its essence. The situation is the reverse of what those thinkers imagine: the cause concerns **existence as such**. If prior nonexistence happens to precede it, it is originated; otherwise, it is not originated.\n\n**§21 [263].** What ordinary people call an “agent” is not, strictly speaking, a cause precisely *in the respect in which they call it an agent*. They regard someone as an agent insofar as it is assumed that previously the agent was not acting. It is therefore called “agent” on account of being a cause *together with* an accompanying circumstance. The consideration of what receives causal existence is conjoined with consideration of what the thing does *not* receive from the cause, and the composite consideration is called “agency.”\n\n**§22 [263].** For that reason, in everything they call an agent, they require it first to have been a nonagent, and afterward to will, undergo compulsion, or acquire some new condition that it previously lacked. When this new circumstance accompanies its essence, the essence-plus-circumstance becomes an actual cause, whereas previously it lacked the condition. They therefore speak of an agent as one who comes to be an actual cause after being only a potential cause, not simply as an actual cause.\n\n**§23 [263].** Everything they call an agent will in that sense also have to be what they call something acted upon, since they associate it with an adventitious state because of which what proceeds from it now exists after not having existed. But it has been shown that the existence of an essence depends on another insofar as it is the *existence of that essence*, not insofar as it is existence after nonexistence. This existence, therefore, is an effect **for as long as it exists**. As an effect, it remains dependent upon another. It is evident that an effect needs the giver of its existence *essentially because of its existence itself*; origination and other such features are accidents belonging to it. The effect therefore needs the giver of its existence always and perpetually, **for as long as it exists**.\n\n**End of VI.1.** The source next begins Book VI, chapter 2, whose full continuous translation is [Fascicle B](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md).\n\n## Source-control notes and substantive issues\n\n1. **Witness status.** These are printed Arabic pages 257–263. The edition reports variant readings, but no manuscript images were inspected or collated independently. The translator's 23 numbered units do not purport to reproduce an Arabic author's 23 original numbered paragraphs.\n2. **Metaphysical versus physical agent (§3).** Ibn Sīnā sharply distinguishes *mabdaʾ al-wujūd wa-mufīduhu* from a merely moving principle in natural philosophy. This does not deny real physical efficacy; rather, it distinguishes the respects in which the agent makes something move or gives it complete being.\n3. **Five versus four causes (§5).** The apparently fifth cause is a receptive *subject* distinct from constituent matter; both can be grouped under material causality due to potentiality. This distinction is especially relevant to debates over whether a subject of an accident has the same ontological role as prime matter.\n4. **Partial role of form (§8).** Avicenna says form appears like *part* of the efficient cause in relation to matter because other principles bring the form into actuality; it is not an unmediated self-sufficient bestower. This anticipates IX.5's joint account of separate-bestowed form and natural preparation.\n5. **“After nonexistence” (§§12–15).** The claim that the temporal *order* of existence after nonexistence has no independent cause **does not** imply that a generated being's existence is causeless. Precisely the contrary: the existence that happens to follow nonexistence is an effect. The argument prevents confusing the accidental temporal predicate with the proper object of the efficient causal act.\n6. **Continuing conservation (§§16–23).** The rejection of “once made, it needs no cause” is directly attested in VI.1. This predates Narāqī and must not be presented as a late innovation. Compare Aquinas *ST* I.104 and *De potentia* 5, distinguishing natural generators from essential preserving causes.\n7. **The “necessity through another” regress (§18).** The argumentative branch is not a blanket rejection of any infinite *temporal* succession; VI.2 later explicitly permits the possibility of unbounded successive preparatory causes. The rejected explanatory option is an infinite set of merely contingent properties pretending to explain existential necessity without an external ground.\n8. **Editorial distinction:** a complete chapter translated into new English prose here is **not** a manuscript-critical Arabic edition, a translation of all of the *Shifāʾ*, or proof of historical identity with Aquinas. The detailed Arabic base can be checked from the cited edited witness.\n\n## Parallel loci\n\n- [VI.2 — essential and preparatory causes](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md)\n- [IX.5 — Giver of Forms](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_D_AVICENNA_IX5.md)\n- [Lāhījī — intrinsic and extrinsic preference](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_C_LAHIJI_PREFERENCE.md)\n- [Existing Chapter 10 — conservation and secondary agency](chapters/10-conservation-first-cause-and-secondary-agency.md)\n\n**Completion stamp:** continuous translation of 23/23 editorial argument units covering the authorial text of VI.1, printed pp.257–263. **Primary Arabic witness:** edited accessible transcription; no independent manuscript collation.\n","sourceUrl":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=257&type=c_fbook","sourceUrls":["https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=257&type=c_fbook"],"sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_E_AVICENNA_VI1.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"av-vi2","file":"PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md","author":"Ibn Sīnā","work":"al-Shifāʾ · Ilāhiyyāt","title":"Essential and preparatory causes","locus":"VI.2 · pp. 263–267","lang":"ar","family":"Classical Avicennism","kind":"primary","coverage":"bounded chapter · working English","topics":["causation","temporal","conservation","motion","form"],"source":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=264&type=c_fbook","md":"# Primary translation fascicle B — Ibn Sīnā, *al-Shifāʾ, al-Ilāhiyyāt* VI.2\n\n**GNAMMA working English translation · 31 October 2026**  \n**Unit translated:** the entire consecutive authorial prose of Book VI, chapter 2, from the objection about the father, builder and fire (printed p. 263) to the paragraph about the agent by essence and by a power (printed p. 267), **ending immediately before chapter 3**. This is not all of Book VI, and not a new Arabic critical edition.\n\n**Base witness:** G. C. Anawati / Saʿīd Zāyid edition, as reproduced with manuscript sigla in the searchable [Rafed text, pp. 263–267](https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=264&type=c_fbook). The transcription carries an editorial apparatus; spelling and punctuation have not been independently established by manuscript collation. [Direct printed page 263](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/263) · [264](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/264) · [265](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/265) · [266](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/266) · [267](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/267).\n\nThe division into numbered paragraphs is editorial, as are the subheadings. The source chapter's printed footnotes are **not** reproduced as a full apparatus; a select reading report follows.\n\n## Continuous translation\n\n### [263] I. What it means for a cause to accompany its effect\n\n**Chapter Two: Resolving the difficulties raised against the position of those who hold the truth—that every cause accompanies its effect—and determining the account of the efficient cause.**\n\n**§1.** The supposition that a son remains after his father, a building after its builder, and heat after fire arises from confusion caused by ignorance of the cause in the proper sense. The builder, father, and fire are not, strictly speaking, causes of the constitution and continued subsistence of these effects. The craftsman who constructs the building is neither the cause of the building's continuing constitution nor, in this proper sense, of its existence.\n\n**§2.** In the case of construction, the builder's motion is the cause of another motion; subsequently, his coming to rest, leaving off movement, or no longer moving and transferring [the material] after that transfer is the cause of the cessation of the movement. That very transfer and the cessation of that movement cause a certain coming together. The coming together causes a certain configuration. In each case, whatever is a cause accompanies its effect.\n\n**§3.** The father, likewise, is a cause of the movement of the semen. When that movement ends in the manner described, it is the cause of the semen's settling in its receptacle. Its settling there is then the cause of something further. As for its being formed into an animal and its continuing to be an animal, these have another cause. Once the matter is understood this way, every cause accompanies its effect.\n\n**§4.** Fire likewise causes the heating of the element water. Heating causes the destruction of the water's actual preparedness to receive the watery form—or to retain it. Another thing then causes the production, in such circumstances, of complete preparedness to receive the opposite form, namely the fiery form. The cause of the fiery form is the causes that clothe the elements in their forms: these are separate [from matter].\n\n### [264] II. Essential and preparatory causes\n\n**§5.** Thus the true causes exist together with their effects. The earlier causes are causes either accidentally or as assistants. It must therefore be held that the cause of a building's shape is the coming together [of its components]; the cause of that is the natures of the things brought together and their remaining as they have become arranged; and the cause of that is the separate cause which produces those natures. The cause of the child is the conjunction of its form with its matter through the cause that bestows forms. The cause of fire is the cause that bestows forms together with the disappearance of the complete preparedness for the opposite of those forms. We find, therefore, that causes are together with their effects.\n\n**§6.** When we judge, in the matters relevant to our discussion, that causes are finite, we are referring to these causes. We do not deny that auxiliary and preparatory causes may proceed without end, one preceding another. Indeed this is necessarily so: every newly occurring thing becomes necessary after not having been necessary because its cause then became necessary, as we have explained; and the cause of that thing, too, became necessary. Consequently, among particular events, the preceding conditions through which actually existing causes become necessitating and become actual causes of their effects can form an unending succession. For this reason the question “Why?” never reaches a halt among them.\n\n**§7.** But there is a difficulty here. Of these things without end, either each exists only for an instant, so that contiguous instants succeed one another with no time between them—which is impossible—or each lasts through a period of time. In that case it ought to necessitate its effect throughout that whole period, rather than at one of its boundaries; and whatever makes it necessitate its effect would also have to accompany it throughout that period. The question of what necessitates its necessitation would recur, yielding an infinity of causes simultaneously.\n\n**§8.** It is precisely this last result that we deny. We say that, were it not for motion, this difficulty would follow. But motion keeps one and the same thing from remaining in one and the same condition. What is renewed, state after state, at each posited instant, does not succeed by discrete instants that abut one another; the renewal is continuous. Thus the cause's essence does not necessitate [the effect] simply through its existence—\n\n### [265] III. Motion resolves the apparent infinite simultaneous series\n\n**§9.** —but through its being in a certain relation. Motion is the cause of that relation, or a partner in its cause; alternatively, motion is what makes the cause an actual cause. The cause is therefore neither fixed in existence in one unchanging state nor altogether deprived of existence and newly originated within a single instant. Necessarily, then, what preserves or shares in preserving the order of these causes, and thereby resolves the difficulties, is motion. We shall make this still clearer elsewhere.\n\n**§10.** It is thus evident that a thing's essential causes, through which its very being exists in actuality, must exist along with it. They must not precede it in existence in such a way that they disappear when the effect comes to be. Such earlier and disappearing causes are permissible only among nonessential or nonproximate causes. There is no obstacle to the nonessential or nonproximate causes extending without end; rather, such a succession is required.\n\n**§11.** Once this is settled, if something is by its own essence always a cause of another thing's existence, it will always cause that thing so long as its own essence exists. If it exists perpetually, its effect will exist perpetually. This is more deserving of the name “cause,” for it excludes sheer nonexistence from the effect and gives it complete existence. Philosophers call this *ibdāʿ* [origination without a pre-existing material subject]: making a thing be after absolute not-being. For the effect, considered in itself, has “not-being”; through its cause, it has “being.”\n\n**§12.** What belongs to a thing in itself is prior in the intellect by essence—not temporally—to what belongs to it through another. Every effect is thus “being after not-being” in an order of essential, rather than temporal, posteriority.\n\n**§13.** If the name “originated” (*muḥdath*) is applied to everything whose “being” is posterior to its “not-being,” even where the posteriority is not temporal, then every effect is originated. But if it is not so applied, because being originated requires a time or moment before which [the thing did not exist]—\n\n### [266] IV. Essential and temporal posteriority; *ibdāʿ* and *takwīn*\n\n**§14.** —so that it did not exist and then came after that interval, its posteriority is a posteriority that does not coexist with the prior state but is distinct from it in existence, precisely because it is temporal. In this sense not every effect is originated. Rather, an originated effect is one whose existence is preceded by a time. That time's passage necessarily involves motion and change, as you already know. We have no wish to quarrel over names.\n\n**§15.** Furthermore, what is originated in the sense that does not require time either has existence after absolute “not-being” or has existence after “not-being” that is not absolute, but is the negation of a particular opposed state in pre-existing matter, as already explained. If its existence follows absolute “not-being,” the proceeding from its cause is *ibdāʿ*. This is the most excellent way of giving existence: nonexistence is wholly excluded and existence established in dominion over it. Were nonexistence to be admitted so that it preceded existence [temporally], the thing could come to be only from matter. The power of bringing into being—by which I mean one thing's existing from another—would then be weaker, limited, and renewed.\n\n**§16.** Some do not call everything with this characteristic “originated by *ibdāʿ*.” They say: if we suppose something to exist from a first cause through an intermediate efficient cause, even without matter and with no prior dominance of nonexistence, then because it receives its existence from the genuinely first cause only after the existence of another thing through which it proceeds, its being-made-to-be is not from absolute “not-being” but from “being,” even though it is not material. Others apply *ibdāʿ* to every instance of formal existence, whatever it may be, whereas in a material effect—even if the matter was not temporally prior—they reserve the name *takwīn* [formation/generation] for its relation to its cause.\n\n**§17.** We certainly do not quarrel over these names, provided the meanings are distinguished. We find that some things always have existence from a cause without matter, and others with matter; some through an intermediary and others without one. It is appropriate to call anything that has not existed out of antecedent matter “not generated” but “originated by *ibdāʿ*,” and to reserve the most excellent sense of *ibdāʿ* for what has no intermediary between it and its first cause—whether that intermediary would have been material, efficient, or something else.\n\n### [267] V. The natural agent\n\n**§18.** Let us return to our subject. The agent to which acting belongs adventitiously must have matter in which to act, for every newly occurring thing, as you have learned, requires matter. It may act at once or by producing motion, in which case it is a principle of motion. When natural philosophers call an agent a “principle of motion,” they mean the four kinds of motion; they speak loosely in this context by also counting generation and corruption as motion.\n\n**§19.** An agent may act by its very essence, or it may act by a power. An agent acting by essence would be like heat if heat existed separately and acted: what proceeded from it would proceed because it was heat, and for no other reason. An agent acting by a power is like fire acting through its heat. We have enumerated the kinds of powers elsewhere.\n\n**End of VI.2.** The next heading in the base witness is “Chapter Three: The correspondence between efficient causes and their effects.”\n\n## Translation apparatus: textual and philosophical issues\n\n1. **Printed-page boundaries.** The chapter heading begins on printed p. 263 and the final two paragraphs stand on p. 267. The website's chapter URL begins at p. 264; its continuous text includes the preceding page. The edition's pagination is more precise than the website URL alone.\n2. **The builder:** `qiwām` is “constitution/continued subsistence,” not simply “coming to be.” The house can remain when the craftsman is absent because the craftsman is not a continually sustaining existential cause in the relevant sense.\n3. **The father:** the passage denies that the father's activity alone is the adequate sustaining cause of the living animal; it does **not** deny reproductive agency or physical causation.\n4. **The Giver of Forms:** the phrase “the causes that clothe the elements in their forms, which are separate” occurs inside §4. The causal role is ontological and natural-philosophical, not merely an allegory of conceptual abstraction. The phrase “cause which bestows forms” in §5 reinforces this.\n5. **Manuscript/print variants (reported by the printed editors, not independently collated):** p. 263 note 19 records `الماء` versus `النار` for the element acted upon; “water” is the main printed reading, while “fire” is assigned to witness *m*. Also at p. 263 n.20, `أو حفظها` / `وحفظها` differs. On p. 265 n.17, `تأييس` (making be) is reported as `تليس` in witness *b*. On p. 265 n.11 the printed `لا متقدمة` is `لا تتقدمه` in several witnesses. These bear on translation but do not justify claims about archetypal readings without the actual manuscripts.\n6. **Motion and infinity:** §§6–10 permit a temporal, causal chain of successive preparatory conditions while denying infinitely many simultaneously existing *essential* causes. The continuity of motion allows changing causal relations without introducing an infinity of coexisting discrete instants. This must not be flattened into “Avicenna rejects every actual infinity” or into an argument for a temporally first event.\n7. **`ibdāʿ`:** rendering it merely “creation” would obscure the distinction from temporal creation and from *takwīn*. The famous `taʾyīs` formula means “making-to-be,” not first supplying a positive entity called non-being.\n8. **`al-muḥdath`:** §§12–14 explicitly distinguish origin in essence from origin in time. This is relevant to Aquinas' account of the possibility of an eternal created world but does not make the two theologies identical on divine freedom.\n9. **Significant doctrinal tension:** §§4–5 assign substantial form-bestowal to separate causes; §§18–19 still recognise material agents, motions, powers, and physical effects. Thus “Avicenna denies natural efficacy” is false, while “Avicenna gives the natural generator the same substantial-form-producing role as Aquinas” remains unproved.\n10. **Scholarly limits:** This file gives a new full continuous translation of *VI.2* as a bounded unit, but it does not independently re-edit the Arabic, reproduce every editorial variant, or translate all of *al-Ilāhiyyāt*. Comparison with printed, scanned and manuscript witnesses remains a separate task.\n\n## Argument map\n\n| Logical step | Thesis | Implication |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | An artisan or parent may be absent while the effect persists | Earlier *becoming* is not identical to an abiding existential cause |\n| 2 | Natural actions produce real motions, assemblages and dispositions | The proximate world is not causally inert |\n| 3 | Intrinsic form and its continuance call for proximate essential causes | The causal explanation is hierarchically layered |\n| 4 | Successive preparatory conditions can be indefinite | Temporal succession is not the same as a simultaneous essential regress |\n| 5 | Motion differentiates causal relations continuously | Causal temporality cannot be modeled as a string of discrete instants |\n| 6 | Perpetual essential cause may have perpetual effect | Ontological origination and temporal initiation differ |\n| 7 | *Ibdāʿ* and *takwīn* refer to different causal modes | “Creation” is not a single undifferentiated concept |\n\n## Relations to the working summa\n\n- [Chapter 05 — Four causes and form-bestowal](chapters/05-four-causes-form-bestowal.md)\n- [Chapter 10 — Creation, conservation and secondary agency](chapters/10-conservation-first-cause-and-secondary-agency.md)\n- [Cumulative synthesis](AT_AVICENNIAN_FINDINGS_1.md)\n- [Primary-source crosswalk](PRIMARY_TEXT_CROSSWALK_01.md)\n\n**Source-critical declaration:** primary Arabic authorial text encountered via an edited searchable transcript; **full contiguous English translation of this one bounded chapter completed**, with source-selected variants reported but no original manuscript collation. This is not a claim of complete twenty-domain translation coverage.\n","sourceUrl":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=264&type=c_fbook","sourceUrls":["https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=264&type=c_fbook"],"sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"av-ix5","file":"PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_D_AVICENNA_IX5.md","author":"Ibn Sīnā","work":"al-Shifāʾ · Ilāhiyyāt","title":"The Giver of Forms and natural generation","locus":"IX.5 · pp. 409–413","lang":"ar","family":"Classical Avicennism","kind":"primary","coverage":"bounded chapter · working English","topics":["forms","natural-generation","intellect","cosmology","causation"],"source":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=410&type=c_fbook","md":"# Primary translation fascicle D — Ibn Sīnā, *al-Shifāʾ: al-Ilāhiyyāt* IX.5\n\n**GNAMMA working translation · 31 October 2026**  \n**Scope:** entire consecutive authorial prose of Book IX, chapter 5, from the heading **في حال تكون الأسطقسات عن العلل الأوائل** (“Concerning the production of the elements from the first causes”) at printed p.409 to the closing criticism of an alternative account of elemental origins on printed p.413. The chapter ends before IX.6 on providence. The division below into thematic sections and numbered paragraphs is editorial. Original marginal variants have *not* been independently collated against manuscripts.\n\n**Source witness:** Ibn Sīnā, *al-Shifāʾ: al-Ilāhiyyāt*, ed. Georges C. Anawati and Saʿīd Zāyid, as reproduced by [Rafed, IX.5, pp.409–413](https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=410&type=c_fbook), printed pp. 409–413. The host reproduces an Arabic text with editorial variant sigla; the text translated here is the main printed reading. Compare the previous chapter IX.4 (necessary hierarchy of separate intellects) and the causal taxonomy in VI.2.\n\n## A. The sublunary elements and their causes — pp.409–410\n\n**§1 [409].** When the celestial spheres have reached their full number, the existence of the elements must follow. For elemental bodies undergo generation and corruption; accordingly, their proximate principles must be things receptive of some kind of alteration and motion. A purely separate intellect, taken by itself, cannot be the sole cause of their existence. This must be established from principles that we have repeated often enough and whose demonstration we have already completed.\n\n**§2 [409].** These elements have a matter that they share and forms through which they differ. The diversity of their forms must therefore be assisted by diversity among the states of the celestial spheres, and their agreement in matter assisted by what is common among the states of the spheres.\n\n**§3 [409–410].** The spheres agree in a nature requiring circular motion. What this common nature requires must therefore assist the existence of [elemental] matter, while their points of difference are principles of matter's preparedness for diverse forms. But multiple things sharing a species or genus do not, without the cooperation of some determinate one, constitute the cause of an entity which is itself one and uniform; the entity's constitution comes from something else. Accordingly, this single thing cannot arise from them except through their being joined to a unity that reduces them to one order.\n\n**§4 [409–410].** The separate intellects—or, rather, the last among them, the one nearest us—must therefore be that from which, *in cooperation with the celestial motions*, there flows something possessing an imprint of the forms of the lower world in the manner of receptivity. In that intellect, or those intellects, the forms have their imprint in the manner of agency. Then the forms flow from it into [receptive matter], each specifically determined, not through the separate intellect's essence acting in isolation. For, as you know, insofar as one acts upon one, only one proceeds from it. Rather, it acts in cooperation with celestial bodies. When this receptive thing is singled out by a celestial influence, either immediately, without an elemental body mediating, or through such a body's mediation, it acquires a special preparedness in addition to the general preparedness that already belonged to its substance. A particular form then emanates from the separate principle and is imprinted in that matter.\n\n## B. Disposition, specificity and the form of fire — p.410\n\n**§5 [410].** You know that one thing, considered precisely as one, cannot single out another thing, considered as one, for one determination rather than another. Distinct differentiating factors are needed. In the case of matter those differentiating factors are its preparations. A preparatory cause is one from which a certain state arises in the subject being prepared, such that the subject's suitability in view of that state is greater for one definite thing than for another. This preparation makes it more fitting that the thing for which it has become especially prepared should exist through the first principles that bestow forms.\n\n**§6 [410].** Were matter left in its initial preparedness, its relation to two contrary forms would be alike. What would determine one rather than the other? Perhaps a difference in the influences acting upon it. But that difference itself would stand in the same relation to all the portions of matter; one portion would not receive its influence rather than another unless there were something further in that portion of matter. That can only be the complete preparedness. Complete preparedness is simply the complete suitability of something for the particular determination for which it is prepared.\n\n**§7 [410].** An example is water heated beyond measure. The foreign heat and the watery form are now found together, even though [this newly introduced heat] has little suitability to the watery form and a strong suitability to the fiery form. Once the heating becomes excessive, that suitability increases and preparedness grows stronger. It then becomes fitting that the fiery form should emanate and that the preceding [watery] form should cease. Since matter cannot remain without a form, its subsistence does not derive from what is attributed to the first principles alone but from those principles **together with form**.\n\n## C. The ontological constitution of matter — p.411\n\n**§8 [411].** Since the form now constituting this matter is one without which the matter had previously existed [when constituted by a different form], matter's subsistence does not come from this form alone; it comes from it, and from the abiding principles, through its mediation or the mediation of another form like it. If [matter's constitution] derived from the first principles alone, it would have no need of form; if from this form alone, it would not have preceded that form. Just as, in the celestial realm, the common element in circular motion requires a nature established by the specific natures of each sphere, so here matter is constituted, together with the common nature, by what proceeds from the specific natures, namely the forms.\n\n**§9 [411].** Just as motion is the lowest of the states in the celestial realm, matter here is the lowest of substances. As motion there is attendant upon the nature of what is in potency, so matter here accompanies what is in potency. As the celestial natures, common and particular, are principles of or assistants to the natures, common and particular, here below, so the diverse and changing relations arising from those celestial natures through motion are principles of the changes and alterations of states here below. Likewise, the intermixture of their relations there is a cause of, or an assistant to, the intermixture of the relations of these elements.\n\n**§10 [411].** The heavenly bodies influence the bodies of this world by the properties peculiar to them, which extend down into this world. The celestial souls likewise influence the souls belonging to this world. It is through these considerations that one knows that the nature governing these bodies through perfection and form comes from, or is assisted by, the soul pervading the heavenly sphere.\n\n## D. Criticism of a purely mechanical theory of elemental origin — pp.412–413\n\n**§11 [412].** Some of those who claim affiliation with this science have said: because the heavenly sphere is circular, it must revolve around something fixed in its interior. Friction against that thing necessarily heats it until it becomes fire. What lies far from the sphere remains motionless, cools, condenses and becomes earth. What lies near the fire is hot, but less hot than fire; what lies near earth is dense, but less dense than earth.\n\n**§12 [412].** They go on: a lesser degree of heat and a lesser degree of condensation give rise to moisture, since dryness derives either from heat or from cold. But the moist element next to earth is colder, while the moist element next to fire is hotter. Such, on their view, is the explanation of the coming-to-be of the elements.\n\n**§13 [412].** This account cannot be demonstrated by syllogistic reasoning; on examination it is unsound. Things seem more likely to follow a different order. The matter resulting from the cooperation [of causes] may receive an influence from the celestial bodies, either from four bodies or from a limited number grouped into four classes. Each of them supplies what prepares the matter for the form of a simple body. Once the matter becomes prepared, it receives the form from the **Giver of Forms**. Alternatively, all this may flow from a single heavenly body, while a cause hidden from us produces its division.\n\n**§14 [412–413].** If you wish to see the weakness of their account, consider what they must suppose: first there is a body that has no constitutive form except corporeal form itself, and only afterward does it acquire the remaining forms through motion and rest. But we have already shown the impossibility of this. We have demonstrated that a body does not acquire complete existence through corporeal form alone without a further form accompanying it. The form which constitutes prime matter is not merely the dimensions, for dimensions themselves depend for their existence upon other forms prior to dimension.\n\n**§15 [412–413].** If you wish, consider rarefaction through heat and condensation through cold. Indeed, a body would not become a body at all, on the account they propose, until it were placed in a condition to follow another [body] in motion, so that its following the successive motions which we have shown to be natural rather than forced would heat it. But before any of that can occur its nature must already be complete. It is, however, possible that after its nature is complete, it is preserved in the location best suited to its preservation: the hot is preserved where motion occurs, and the cold where there is rest.\n\n**§16 [413].** Nor do these thinkers consider why one part of that matter should sink toward the centre and thereby become cold, while another should pass upward. We have already identified the reason for this: on the general level, lightness and heaviness; and in the case of a particular part of one element, the fact that the elements' parts undergo generation. If some part comes into being in a particular place, necessarily one of its surfaces is toward the upper region. If it moves upward, that surface has a greater claim to being uppermost than the other surface. At its first coming-to-be, however, one surface faces upward and another downward because the part has necessarily undergone some change involving motion, which has imposed a definite spatial orientation.\n\n**§17 [413].** What we ourselves have maintained therefore seems closer to the truth. I suspect that whoever originally advanced that explanation of the origin of the elements wished to render the matter intelligible to one of his ordinary correspondents, but those who came later treated the remark as a definitive doctrine. In any case, the writer of that account is greatly given to wavering and confusion.\n\n**End of IX.5.** The next heading, printed p.413, introduces IX.6 on providence and the presence of evil in divine decree.\n\n## Selected Arabic anchors and translation controls\n\n| Printed page | Arabic anchor | Rendering | Editorial reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 409 | **وأن لا يكون ما هو عقل محض وحده سببا لوجودها** | “A purely separate intellect, taken alone, cannot be the sole cause of their existence” | Natural mediation is expressly needed |\n| 409–410 | **بمشاركة الحركات السماوية** | “in cooperation with the celestial motions” | Form bestowal does not replace all other causes |\n| 410 | **ومخصصات المادة معداتها** | “matter's differentiators are its preparatory causes” | Relates *takhṣīṣ* to *iʿdād* |\n| 410 | **الاستعداد الكامل** | “complete preparedness” | Differentiates initial generic receptivity from determinate suitability |\n| 410 | **أن تفيض ... أن تبطل** | “emanate ... cease” | Both generation and passing of the previous form |\n| 411 | **فليس قوامها ... بل بها وبالمبادئ الباقية** | “matter's subsistence comes from both the form and the abiding principles” | Distinguishes ontological constitution from temporal preparation |\n| 412 | **فإذا استعد نال الصورة من واهب الصور** | “when prepared, it receives the form from the Giver of Forms” | Explicit source identification |\n| 412 | **لا يستكمل له وجود بمجرد الصورة الجسمية** | “it is not fully constituted merely by corporeal form” | Anti-reductionist hylomorphism |\n\n## Editorial notes and reported variants\n\n1. **Textual level.** The page presents an edited text and reported witness readings. We have translated the printed main reading, not compared manuscript codices. Several printed variant notes affect theological terminology, and none should be described as a critical emendation made in this edition.\n2. **The subject of form-reception.** §4 has a potentially unstable pronoun chain: the separate intellect, receptive matter and imprinted forms are distinguished by the parallel between *bi-jihat al-infiʿāl* (“receptive”) and *bi-jihat al-tafʿīl* (“active”). The translation inserts “[receptive matter]” for clarity. Collate the Arabic and the Latin *Avicenna Latinus* witnesses before calling the pronoun referents definitive.\n3. **A substantive variant.** At printed p.410 n.22, the water example reads **الماء** in the base printed text while **المادة** (“matter”) is reported for witness *d*. This affects the level of concreteness, not the form-bestowal doctrine. At p.410 n.23, **المائية** (“watery”) has **الماهية** (“quiddity”) in witness *d*, almost certainly a scribal or transcription confusion but not silently corrected here.\n4. **“Complete” preparedness.** The adjective **الكامل** is reported omitted by several witnesses at p.410 n.18. The argument still requires a greater-than-generic disposition, but the explicit formula is sensitive to witness choice.\n5. **Sublunary and celestial causal levels.** The author attributes real physical *influences* to the heavenly bodies, not only intelligible exemplarity to separate intellects. A historically accurate comparison must preserve that division of labour.\n6. **Necessary qualification of §8.** The claim that matter existed “without this form” is consistent with the earlier claim that matter never exists *without any form*. It existed together with another constituting form. Confusing “this form” with “every form” would create a contradiction that the text explicitly rejects.\n7. **“Giver of Forms” is not Aquinas's substantial generator.** The separate source bestows a form intrinsic to the newly constituted composite, whereas Aquinas ordinarily assigns the generation of the material composite to the natural agent, describing the form as educed from the potency of matter. Their disagreement remains real though their hylomorphic explanations share matter, form, preparation and final order.\n8. **On §15.** The passage around **لا يصير جسما حتى يصير بحيث يتبع غيره** is compressed and has a Latin-version note at p.412 n.33. The English retains the order of Avicenna's reductio rather than asserting that an element cannot move before it exists.\n9. **Boundary of the claim.** This is **all of IX.5 translated into English**, but not all of *Ilāhiyyāt* IX, all of natural philosophy, or all of the primary witnesses. The chapter's Arabic is available through the linked digital edition; this file is a translator's continuous English text with Arabic lemmata, not a reprinted full bilingual diplomatic text.\n\n## Relation to other fascicles and chapters\n\n- [Avicenna, *Ilāhiyyāt* VI.2 — complete bounded translation](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md)\n- [Lāhījī, *Shawāriq* I:90–93 — preference, cause and necessity](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_C_LAHIJI_PREFERENCE.md)\n- [Existing Chapter 05 — four causes and form bestowal](chapters/05-four-causes-form-bestowal.md)\n- [Existing Chapter 10 — conservation and secondary agency](chapters/10-conservation-first-cause-and-secondary-agency.md)\n- [Cumulative source synthesis](AT_AVICENNIAN_FINDINGS_1.md)\n\n**Completion stamp:** English translation **IX.5 17/17 bounded authorial units**, printed pp.409–413. **Witness status:** searchable edited Arabic transcription with selected variants; independent manuscript collation **not performed**.\n","sourceUrl":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=410&type=c_fbook","sourceUrls":["https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=1128&page=410&type=c_fbook"],"sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_D_AVICENNA_IX5.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"lah-shaw","file":"PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_C_LAHIJI_PREFERENCE.md","author":"ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī","work":"Shawāriq al-ilhām","title":"Intrinsic preference and causal necessitation","locus":"I:90–93 · issues 23–24","lang":"ar","family":"Post-Tajrīd","kind":"primary","coverage":"two bounded issues · working English","topics":["modality","causation","necessity","divine-will"],"source":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/90?lang=ar","md":"# Primary translation fascicle C — Lāhījī, *Shawāriq al-ilhām* I:90–93\n\n**GNAMMA working critical translation · 31 October 2026**\n\n**Bounded authorial text translated:** Issue 23, “On denying intrinsic preference” (*nafy al-awlawiyya al-dhātiyya*), **from its heading at printed I:90 through its concluding sentence at I:93**, followed by Issue 24, “A contingent thing does not occur until one alternative becomes necessary through its cause,” **from its heading at I:93 through the close of Lāhījī's argument on intrinsic and extrinsic preference, before the next lemma on antecedent and consequent necessity**. Page boundaries below are explicit; the source's punctuation has been regularised in translation. Arabic source headings and short anchor phrases are given here; the full original text is accessible at each page link.\n\n**Witness:** ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī, *Shawāriq al-ilhām fī sharḥ Tajrīd al-kalām*, vol. I, Isfahan, Mahdawī publishing, searchable print-transcription [p. 90](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/90?lang=ar), [p. 91](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/91?lang=ar), [p. 92](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/92?lang=ar), [p. 93](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/93?lang=ar). This copy is not an independently collated manuscript. A few bracketed editorial readings and transcription faults are noted below.\n\n## Issue 23 — Denying preference intrinsic to a contingent being\n\nArabic heading: **المسألة الثالثة والعشرون في نفى الأولوية الذاتيّة ليتبيّن تساوى طرفي الممكن بالنظر إلى ذاته**\n\n### [90] The problem and the rival views\n\n**§23.1** The purpose is to show that the two alternatives for a contingent thing stand on an equal footing in relation to its own essence. That equality did not follow merely from the earlier division [into necessary, impossible and possible], for the possible thing identified in that division was something whose own essence requires neither existence nor nonexistence in such a way that one of them would be necessary for it. This does not by itself exclude the possibility of one alternative being *preferable* for it intrinsically with a preference falling short of necessity.\n\n**§23.2** The majority maintain that the existence and nonexistence of the contingent being are equal in relation to its essence, and that neither alternative has any preference over the other. Some have said that nonexistence is preferable for the contingent being unconditionally. They say so either because they imagine that the occurrence of nonexistence requires no cause, or because fewer conditions are required in the state of nonexistence: the nonexistence of one component of the complete cause of existence is sufficient, and so nonexistence is easier to bring about, whereas existence demands the conjunction of all of them.\n\n**§23.3** The weakness of both considerations is evident. Against the first: just as a contingent thing's existence depends on the existence of its cause, so its nonexistence depends on the absence of that cause. Against the second: the absence of a composite is nothing other than the non-obtaining of all of its components together, whether some components occur or none do. This does not entail an intrinsic preference for nonexistence, as though the contingent thing's essence had a tendency toward nonexistence. In sum, the greater ease of the thing's nonexistence relative to other factors does not entail a preference residing in its own essence.\n\n**§23.4** Others have said that when an efficient agent exists but a condition is missing, existence is preferable to nonexistence for the contingent thing; or that if its cause exists, existence is preferable, and otherwise nonexistence is preferable. The defect in these views is equally obvious: the preference they describe arises from something other than the contingent thing, not from its essence.\n\n**§23.5** Others say that nonexistence is preferable for flowing accidents, such as motion, time and sound, and their qualities, because they cannot persist. This too is unsound. Existence is distinct from persistence and does not entail it. The quiddities of such things, since they entail termination and renewal, are not capable of persistence. Their incapacity for persistence does not amount to an intrinsic preference for never existing.\n\n### [90–91] The commentator on the *Maqāṣid* distinguishes three meanings\n\n**§23.6 — Reported speech of Taftāzānī, explicitly attributed by Lāhījī.** The commentator on the *Maqāṣid* says: Sound reflection requires us to distinguish meanings. If “the preference of existence or nonexistence” means that one alternative becomes preponderant in virtue of the contingent thing's essence in such a way that it actually occurs without an external cause, its impossibility is necessary: the thing would then be necessary or impossible [rather than contingent].\n\n**§23.7** If someone objects that this would follow only if the other alternative could not occur through an external determinant, the reply is that the occurrence of the allegedly preferred alternative would then depend on the absence of that external determinant. [Hence the intrinsic essence would not be sufficient by itself.]\n\n**§23.8** If “preference” means that an alternative is closer to occurring because it needs fewer conditions and impediments or because its causes are more frequently present, this is a preference arising through another, not intrinsic preference. That is clear.\n\n**§23.9** If it means that, when the intellect considers a contingent thing, it may discover in it a certain inclination toward existence or nonexistence, but not one strong enough to produce actual occurrence—for that would turn the contingent thing into a necessary or impossible thing—then its impossibility is not apparent. [End of the threefold distinction.]\n\n**§23.10** He [Taftāzānī] then says that the majority offered various proofs to exclude this third meaning; he sets them out and responds that they depend on building actual occurrence into the meaning. They therefore do not touch the point in dispute.\n\n### [91] Lāhījī determines the point genuinely in dispute\n\n**§23.11 — Lāhījī's own response.** I say: the commentator has treated the impossibility of a preference sufficient for occurrence as self-evident and not open to dispute, while treating as disputable the impossibility of a preference too weak to bring anything about. But the apparent subject of this discussion is the first, not the second. The theologians made this thesis a premise of the contingent thing's need for an efficient cause and accorded it great importance lest the way to proving the Creator be blocked. Indeed, the fact that the proofs intended to disprove intrinsic preference presuppose occurrence is the clearest indication that what is at issue is a preference sufficient for occurrence.\n\n**§23.12** Admittedly, those who call nonexistence preferable to the contingent thing absolutely, or to flowing accidents in particular, appear to intend the third meaning of preference, the one not sufficient for actual occurrence. As for the alleged self-evidence that a preference sufficient for occurrence is impossible, even if we grant that this is necessary, it is not so manifestly necessary that dispute is inconceivable. In fact the proposition that occurrence without an external cause entails that what occurs is necessary or impossible is not itself immediately evident. A necessary being is not simply something needing no other for its existence; it is what needs no other *for the necessity of its existence*. The issue is therefore open to argument, though the true position is close to self-evident.\n\n**§23.13** The author [Ṭūsī] points toward this when he says that it is not conceivable for either alternative of the contingent thing to possess intrinsic preference. By saying “it cannot be conceived,” without offering an explicit demonstration, he indicates that such preference is unintelligible. We can explain this as follows. Suppose a contingent thing has intrinsic preference, meaning that its essence is sufficient for that preference, and suppose that the preference is itself sufficient for one alternative actually occurring. Then either the preference is not mere preference but necessity—in which case the contingent thing is transformed into something necessary or impossible—or else the preference is not intrinsic. Thus a preference that is at once intrinsic and merely preferential is inconceivable.\n\n**§23.14** Here is the entailment. Suppose one alternative enjoys an intrinsic preference. If the arrival of the opposite alternative is impossible, that opposite is impossible and the preferred alternative necessary: the first consequence follows. If the opposite can occur, either it occurs without a cause, entailing that the less-preferred alternative prevails without a cause—worse than one of two equal alternatives prevailing causelessly—or it occurs through a cause. If that cause does not make the opposite preferable, it is not a cause of its occurrence. If it does make the opposite preferable, what was intrinsically preferred has become dispreferred. Its preference, then, was not intrinsic, for what belongs to a thing intrinsically cannot be removed. The second consequence follows.\n\n### [91–92] Objections to the proof and Lāhījī's replies\n\n**§23.15 — Objection.** If the possibility of the opposite alternative's occurring, or its impossibility, is considered relative to the essence of the contingent thing, the claim that something intrinsic cannot disappear follows only if the essence requires its preference *necessarily*. But if the essence requires that preference only preferentially, no such conclusion follows. The opponent does not concede that what is intrinsically preferred cannot change. Indeed, whether a contingent thing can intrinsically prefer one of its alternatives without excluding the other is precisely the disputed question.\n\n**§23.16 — Objection continued.** If, however, the possibility or impossibility of the alternative is considered as a matter of fact, not merely in relation to the essence, we reject the inference from the factual impossibility of the opposite alternative to the necessity of the preferred one. Necessary existence, after all, means that a thing's opposite is impossible with respect to its essence, not absolutely. Here it is possible that the opposite is possible relative to the contingent thing's own essence but impossible relative to the preference the essence has conferred. The essence, through that preference as intermediary, would thus give rise to necessity, while necessity in the earlier division meant what was necessary without reference to anything else. What is necessary relative to another thing—the preference—is not necessary in itself.\n\n**§23.17 — Further objection.** Furthermore, a cause only makes its effect preferable when the cause actually occurs. If the mere essence of the cause, irrespective of its occurrence, sufficed to make the effect preferable, both alternatives of a contingent thing would be preferable at the same time, since each requires a cause, and that is impossible. We may therefore suppose that the cause of the less-preferred alternative never occurs. In that case the lesser alternative never becomes preferred, and the preference intrinsic to the first is not removed. Nor will it suffice to appeal to the *possibility* of the other cause's occurring, for that would make it possible to remove an intrinsic feature, which is also impossible. One could moreover deny that the opposite's cause is itself possible: just as a contingent thing's cause may be necessary in itself, so it may be impossible in itself—for instance the nonexistence of the Necessary Existent in relation to the nonexistence of the First Intellect. [The objection reaches the page-92 boundary.]\n\n**§23.18 — Lāhījī's first answer, [92].** We first choose the reading in which the alternative's possible occurrence is considered relative to the essence of the contingent thing. We say that the essence requires the preference necessarily, given our assumption that the essence is sufficient for producing it. The preference cannot fail to belong to that essence. If it could, either its disappearance would be causeless—making the less-preferred alternative preponderant without a cause—or it would have a cause, in which case the absence of that cause would have played a part in producing the preference and the essence would not have been sufficient for it. That contradicts the supposition.\n\n**§23.19 — Second answer.** We next choose the reading in which the alternative's possibility or impossibility is considered as a matter of fact. To the first objection we reply: when the essence together with a preference grounded in it makes existence necessary, this essence is a principle from which it is impossible for existence to be separated. That is precisely what we mean by necessity; there is no damage to the conclusion from considering an intermediary that derives from the essence.\n\n**§23.20** To the second objection we reply: if the less-preferred alternative is possible, it certainly has a cause, whether that cause is itself possible or impossible. Thus the preference of the supposedly preferred alternative depends upon the absence of the opposite's cause. The essence is not sufficient by itself to produce that preference.\n\n### [92] The last attempted escape\n\n**§23.21 — Objection.** But the preference denied in this argument is one for which the essence is sufficient and which is sufficient to bring the contingent thing into actuality. Someone might therefore propose either a preference for which the essence is *not* sufficient, since it requires the absence of the less-preferred alternative's cause, or a preference for which the essence is sufficient but which is *not* sufficient for actual occurrence, since occurrence additionally requires that absence. Suppose in both cases that the favored alternative is existence and that no cause of nonexistence is present. A contingent thing would then exist without requiring any existing efficient cause, blocking the proof of the Creator just as in the case of a sufficient preference.\n\n**§23.22 — Lāhījī's reply.** I answer: where the essence is not sufficient to account for the preference, or where the preference itself is not sufficient for actual occurrence, the source of the preferred alternative's actuality cannot be the essence alone. Consequently, when the preferred alternative is existence, its actual occurrence plainly requires an existing efficient agent, just as it would if there were no preference at all.\n\n**§23.23** It thus follows that, once a preference sufficient for occurrence has been excluded, the alternatives of a contingent thing are equal in their need for a cause. Positing a preference that falls short of sufficiency makes no difference to their equality in this respect. The majority's position is established: existence and nonexistence are equal in relation to the contingent thing's essence—equal, that is, with respect to occurrence and the requirement for a cause of occurrence—whether a non-sufficient intrinsic preference is postulated or not.\n\n**§23.24 — A further interpretation attributed to “the Master of the Critical Investigators.”** That scholar construes intrinsic preference in another way: just as the necessary and impossible are things that have necessity of existence or nonexistence in relation to themselves, rather than being things whose essence *causes* the necessity of either alternative, an opponent might say that a contingent thing exists because existence is, as a matter of fact, more appropriate or fitting for it intrinsically than nonexistence, without the essence exerting any causal influence at all. Proofs framed in terms of the essence's producing or causing a preference cannot rule out this possibility without qualification.\n\n**§23.25** One possible refinement is to say that the essence must be sufficient for the preference, and the preference sufficient for the occurrence of the preferred alternative, whether that sufficiency is causal or merely a matter of concomitance. If the opposite is impossible relative to the essence, the original supposition is contradicted. If it remains possible relative to the essence, then something for whose occurrence the essence is sufficient could fail to accompany the essence. That is impossible in just the way that the effect cannot fail to accompany its complete cause: the impossibility of the latter is grounded in the sufficiency of the complete cause.\n\n**§23.26** A more satisfactory rebuttal, however, is that occurrence without any basis in efficacy or determination has no intelligible meaning. The analogy with a necessary being is unsound: in the necessary existent, existence is identical with the essence, which is why one cannot posit a separate causal determination between them; in the contingent thing [existence is not thus identical]—\n\n### [93] End of Issue 23\n\n**§23.27** —and so the preferred existence cannot be identical with the essence of the contingent thing; otherwise separation from existence would be impossible, and existence would not merely be preferred but necessary. [End of Issue 23.]\n\n## Issue 24 — A possible thing must become necessary through its cause before it occurs\n\nArabic heading: **المسألة الرابعة والعشرون في ان الممكن ما لم يجب وجوده من العلة لم يوجد**\n\n### [93] External preference is conceivable but insufficient\n\n**§24.1** [The heading means:] a contingent thing does not exist until its existence becomes necessary through its cause; similarly it does not fail to exist until its nonexistence becomes necessary through its cause. The author indicates this by saying: *“Extrinsic preference is not sufficient.”* That is, preference arising from a cause external to the essence of the contingent thing does not suffice to bring about either existence or nonexistence; one alternative must become necessary through the cause before it occurs. Extrinsic preference is thus conceivable, but insufficient for occurrence, whereas intrinsic preference sufficient for occurrence was not conceivable.\n\n**§24.2** Extrinsic preference is conceivable because, if we suppose it and suppose that the contingent thing occurs by means of it alone, no transformation of an intrinsically contingent being into an intrinsically necessary one follows, nor does an intrinsically belonging feature disappear. If the opposite alternative cannot occur, the preference need not have become an *intrinsic* necessity, so the reversal of intrinsic modality does not follow: necessity through another is consistent with intrinsic possibility. If the opposite can occur, this involves the disappearance of a feature received through another through the action of another, which is not impossible.\n\n**§24.3** But extrinsic preference is not sufficient. This is what the author indicates in his reason: *“For supposing it does not make the opposite impossible.”* Supposing extrinsic preference for one alternative does not make the opposite alternative impossible. Otherwise it would be extrinsic *necessity*, not extrinsic preference. With that preference, then, the opposite can occur as well as the supposedly preferred alternative. The occurrence and non-occurrence of the favored alternative remain equally possible under the conditions specified; the preference has been included in the complete specification of either outcome, yet has not determined one.\n\n**§24.4** For example, a contingent thing whose existence is favored can still fail to exist, just as it can exist. Neither outcome has been settled by the preference alone. If its existence is supposed to occur solely through that preference, one of two equal alternatives prevails without a determinant. If it occurs through some further determinant, we repeat the question about that determinant. We must finally reach necessity—a determinant through which the contingent thing's existence, for example, becomes necessary—otherwise an infinite regress ensues. More decisively, even allowing the entire infinite sequence of proposed determinants, either their total operation necessitates the contingent thing's existence, which is what we seek, or it does not, in which case we still have a prevailing alternative without a sufficient determinant. Hence one alternative of the possible thing cannot occur until it becomes necessary.\n\n### [93] Jurjānī, Dawwānī and Lāhījī\n\n**§24.5 — Objection of al-Sayyid al-Sharīf al-Jurjānī as attributed in this passage.** The need for a further determinant can be disputed. Why should not the preponderance arising from an external cause suffice for the favored alternative's occurrence? That is not self-evidently impossible; what is self-evidently impossible is the occurrence of one of two equal alternatives or the less favored alternative. [End reported objection.]\n\n**§24.6 — Lāhījī.** From the explanation already given, it is apparent that this objection has no force.\n\n**§24.7 — Jurjānī's alternative argument.** It would be better to say that the cause through which existence occurs must determine existence. Otherwise, with it, existence and nonexistence would both be possible. Suppose existence accompanies it at one time and nonexistence at another. The fact that one alternative occurs at the one time but not the other, if not owing to a determinant absent at the other time, entails the uncaused preponderance of one of two equals.\n\n**§24.8 — Dawwānī's objection to that formulation.** What is possible is a thing whose existence and nonexistence are admissible when considered in relation to its essence, not necessarily something capable of existing at one time and not at another. Such temporal alternation may be impossible even though the thing is contingent, as with time itself. Even if we concede the supposition, one could say that the alternation is impossible in relation to the preference furnished by the cause: if that preference persists throughout the relevant period, existence cannot be restricted to one part and nonexistence to another without an additional differentiating consideration.\n\n**§24.9 — Lāhījī's repair.** I say: this objection does reach the argument in that form. But if we put it instead as follows, it would succeed. If existence is not necessitated by the cause, both existence and nonexistence remain possible in its presence. Thus nonexistence could occur *instead of* existence; the specification of one alternative rather than the other requires a further determinant. The objection based on two different times would then no longer apply.\n\n**§24.10** Note also that this proof against merely extrinsic preference would exclude intrinsic preference too, if intrinsic preference were conceivable, as is clear from what has preceded. [Here the issue's argument ends; the next words in the source begin a further lemma on antecedent and consequent necessity and are not translated in this fascicle.]\n\n## Critical apparatus and argumentative reconstruction\n\n1. **Doctrinal scope.** *Awlawiyya* means a preference or preponderance weaker than *wujūb* (necessity). The question is **not** whether some physical conditions make an event statistically likelier. It concerns whether preference, taken as metaphysically insufficient for necessity, can **fully explain actual existence**.\n2. **The decisive distinction in Issue 23:** “not necessary” leaves open (a) a merely conceptual preference, (b) a preference aided by extrinsic conditions, and (c) an intrinsic preference itself sufficient for occurrence. The objection to the argument for a First Cause principally targets (c). Lāhījī recognizes that (a) may be noncontradictory yet irrelevant to causal sufficiency.\n3. **Speech layers:** Lāhījī quotes or paraphrases Taftāzānī's *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, Ṭūsī's *Tajrīd*, Jurjānī and Dawwānī. Their speeches above are translated **as reported by Lāhījī**, not as independently verified versions of those works. This prevents false witness-counting.\n4. **“The Master of the Critical Investigators”** is retained as an unattributed title in §23.24. Its named identity must be established by collating the wider witness and scholastic usage; it is unsafe to assign it to a particular individual here.\n5. **Printed/transcription difficulty, p.92:** the sentence transcribed as `أو الأولوية كافية الوقوع` sits inside a reply that logically requires **“or the preference is insufficient for occurrence”**; the preceding objection and the following conclusion make this apparent. The English represents the argumentative reading, but no silent Arabic emendation is claimed: verify the printed scan and manuscript before choosing between `غير كافية` and other possibilities.\n6. **Further p.92 difficulty:** `أو الأولوية كافية الوقوع` and several unpointed forms in this searchable text underscore the need to distinguish translation of a plausible authorial reading from a settled critical text. Treat this as a **working edition**.\n7. **Lāhījī's result is not a proof of determinism in ordinary physics.** The conditional maxim is: given the *complete* cause, one alternative must be necessitated. Whether “complete cause” includes divine volition, conditions, and impediments remains a distinct argument.\n8. **The Ibn Sīnā connection:** compare *Ilāhiyyāt* I.6, IV.1, VI.1–2 and the later *Ishārāt* proof. His necessity-through-another does not simply mean that the quiddity itself becomes intrinsically necessary.\n9. **The Aquinas connection:** distinguish *necessitas consequentiae* (conditional necessity given the complete antecedent) and *necessitas consequentis* (necessity of the resulting thing). The nearest Thomist analogy is a conditional necessity compatible with contingency and, in appropriate contexts, divine free willing. Analogy is not identity.\n10. **Completeness claim limited to these two discrete issues:** the translation is continuous from the heading of Issue 23 to its end and from Issue 24's heading to the end of its actual proof; it is *not* an exhaustive translation of *Shawāriq* I or an independent collation of Taftāzānī, Jurjānī, Dawwānī or Ṭūsī.\n\n## Cross-links\n\n- [The complete English translation of Ibn Sīnā, *Ilāhiyyāt* VI.2](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md)\n- [Existing Lāhījī / Narāqī primary crosswalk](PRIMARY_TEXT_CROSSWALK_01.md)\n- [The causation and regress translation dossier](primary-translation-causation.md)\n- [Chapter 06 — divine agency, necessity and plurality](chapters/06-divine-agency-necessity-and-plurality.md)\n- [Cumulative synthesis](AT_AVICENNIAN_FINDINGS_1.md)\n\n**Status:** complete working English translation of two identified consecutive argument units from a searchable printed witness, with commentary and explicit uncertainty; not manuscript-critical and not a translation of the entire work.\n","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/90?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/90?lang=ar"],"sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_C_LAHIJI_PREFERENCE.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"aq-104a","file":"PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_F_AQUINAS_ST104.md","author":"Thomas Aquinas","work":"Summa theologiae","title":"Conservation and intermediary causes","locus":"I q.104 aa.1–2","lang":"la","family":"Thomist comparison","kind":"primary","coverage":"two complete articles · working English","topics":["conservation","causation","divine-will"],"source":"https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html","md":"# Primary translation fascicle F — Thomas Aquinas, *Summa theologiae* I, q.104, aa.1–2\n\n**GNAMMA new working English translation, 31 October 2026**  \n**Scope:** the **complete two question-and-response articles**, beginning *Utrum creaturae indigeant ut a Deo conserventur in esse* and *Utrum Deus immediate omnem creaturam conservet*, including all four objections, counterauthority, body and four replies of article 1, and all three objections, contrary authority, body and three replies of article 2. Not the whole Question 104 (articles 3–4 treat annihilation), and not the whole *Summa*. Latin anchors and critical distinctions follow; the full source is linked rather than duplicated.\n\n**Latin base:** [Corpus Thomisticum, *Summa theologiae* I, q.104, aa.1–2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html), **Leonine text / Busa–Alarcón**, paragraph IDs **[32757]–[32774]**. Parallel [Latin on Wikisource](https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Summa_Theologiae_prima_pars_quaestio_CIV). This is a fresh working translation from the Latin, not an adaptation of the Dominican Fathers' existing English translation. All numbering below tracks the Latin paragraphs.\n\n## Article 1. Whether creatures need to be conserved in being by God\n\n### Objections\n\n**1. [32757]** It seems that creatures do not need to be kept in existence by God. For what cannot fail to exist needs no conservation in being, just as something that cannot depart needs no one to prevent it from departing. Yet some creatures are such that, according to their natures, they cannot cease to be. Therefore not all creatures need divine conservation. The minor premise is shown as follows: what belongs to a thing of itself must belong to it, and its opposite cannot belong to it; a multiple of two, for example, is necessarily even and cannot be odd. Existence follows form of itself, for everything is an actual being insofar as it has form. Some creatures are subsistent forms, as has been said of angels, so that existence belongs to them of itself. Likewise things whose matter has potency only for one form, such as celestial bodies, according to the older cosmology, exist by necessity of their own natures and cannot cease to be: the capacity not to exist could be grounded neither in form, which existence follows of itself, nor in matter under a form that it cannot lose, since it has potency for no alternative form.\n\n**2. [32758]** Further, God is more powerful than any created agent. But a created agent can impart to its effect the ability to continue in being after the agent's operation ceases: a house remains after the builder's action ceases, and water heated by fire retains heat for a time after the fire is removed. A fortiori, God can grant a creature continued being after divine action has ceased.\n\n**3. [32759]** Further, no violent event occurs without an acting cause. But tending toward nonexistence is unnatural and violent to any creature, since every creature naturally seeks being. No creature, then, can tend toward nonexistence except through something acting to corrupt it. Yet there are things which nothing can corrupt, such as spiritual substances and celestial bodies. Such creatures cannot tend toward nonexistence even if divine action ceases.\n\n**4. [32760]** Further, if God conserves things in being, this will be through some action. Every efficacious action of an agent produces something in the effect. Therefore the conserving action would produce something in the creature. But it apparently does not. It cannot produce the creature's very existence, since what already exists is not made; nor can it add something else, since God would then either not conserve continuously or continually add something to the creature, which is unacceptable. Thus creatures are not conserved by God.\n\n### On the contrary — [32761]\n\nScripture says (Hebrews 1) that the Son sustains all things by the word of his power.\n\n### I respond — [32762]\n\nWe must say, both according to faith and according to reason, that creatures are conserved in being by God. To make this clear we must consider that one thing conserves another in two ways.\n\nOne way is **indirectly and accidentally**, as someone is said to preserve a thing by removing a corrupting cause: a person who prevents a child from falling into fire is said to preserve the child. In this way God may be said to preserve some things, but not all: some things have no corrupting causes that have to be removed in order to conserve them.\n\nA second way is **directly and of itself**, insofar as the conserved thing depends on what preserves it so that it cannot exist without it. In this way every creature needs divine conservation. The being of every creature depends upon God, so that none could subsist even for a moment, but would fall into nothingness, unless conserved by the operation of divine power, as Gregory says.\n\nThis can be seen as follows. Every effect depends on its cause **according to the respect in which it is its cause**. But some agents are causes of their effects only in their **coming-to-be** (*secundum fieri*), not directly in their **being** (*secundum esse*). This happens among both artefacts and natural things.\n\nA builder is the cause of the house's coming-to-be, but not directly of its continuing being. Clearly the house's being follows its form; that form is a composition and ordering which follows the natural powers of certain things. As a cook cooks food by employing a natural active power—fire—so a builder makes a house by employing mortar, stones and timber, whose natures make them capable of receiving and retaining such composition and order. The house's being therefore depends on the natures of these things, while its coming-to-be depends on the builder's operation.\n\nThe same reasoning applies to natural things. If an agent is not cause of **form precisely as this kind of form**, it will not be an essential cause of the existence following that form, but only a cause of the effect's coming-to-be. Clearly, when two things belong to the same species, one cannot be an essential cause of the other's form insofar as it is such a form, since on that account it would be a cause of its own form—the intelligible account of the form is the same in both. Yet it can be a cause of that form **as received in this matter**, that is, of this matter's acquiring the form. Such an agent causes *coming-to-be*: one human being generates another and one fire generates another.\n\nWhenever a natural effect is able to receive the agent's influence in the same respect as it exists in the agent, the effect's generation depends on the agent, but not its being as such. Sometimes, however, an effect cannot receive the agent's influence in the same respect in which it exists in the agent. This is evident when an agent does not produce something like itself in species, as the heavenly bodies were held to cause the generation of inferior things differing from them in species. Such an agent can be a cause of form **under the intelligible account of the form itself**, and not merely as acquired in this matter. It is therefore a cause not only of the thing's coming-to-be but also of its being.\n\nJust as a thing's coming-to-be cannot continue if the agent causing it *as coming-to-be* ceases to act, so the thing's being cannot continue if the action of an agent causing its effect **not only in becoming but in being** ceases. This explains why water retains heat for a time after fire ceases to act upon it, whereas illuminated air does not remain illuminated even for an instant once the sun ceases to shine. Water's matter can receive fire's heat in the same manner in which it exists in fire; if water were brought perfectly into the form of fire it would always retain the heat. If it participates imperfectly in something of the form of fire, by a mere inception, the heat continues for a time but not forever, because the participation in the principle of heat is weak.\n\nAir cannot receive light in the same manner as it exists in the sun, so as to receive the sun's very form, which is the principle of light. Since the light has no intrinsic root of that sort in air, it ceases as soon as the sun's action ceases. **Every creature stands toward God as air toward the illuminating sun.** Just as the sun is luminous by its own nature while the air becomes luminous through sharing in the sun's light without sharing in the sun's nature, so God alone is being through his own essence, since his essence is his existence. Every creature, by contrast, is a being through **participation**, not through an identity between its essence and its being. Augustine therefore says that if God's power ever ceased to govern created things, their species and nature would immediately collapse; and elsewhere that as air shines in the presence of light, so a person is enlightened by God's presence and immediately darkened in his absence.\n\n### Replies\n\n**To objection 1 — [32763].** Existence follows the form of a creature of itself, **on the supposition of God's influx**, just as light follows the translucence of air on the supposition of the sun's influx. Hence the possibility of nonexistence in spiritual creatures and heavenly bodies lies rather in God, who can withhold his influx, than in the form or matter of such creatures.\n\n**To objection 2 — [32764].** God cannot communicate to a creature the power to continue in being when God's operation ceases, any more than he could communicate to it the property of God's not being the cause of its being. The creature requires conservation precisely insofar as the effect's existence depends on the cause of being. An agent who is not the cause of being but only of coming-to-be is a different case.\n\n**To objection 3 — [32765].** The argument concerns conservation by the removal of a corrupting agent. Not all creatures need conservation in that sense, as has been explained.\n\n**To objection 4 — [32766].** God's conservation of things is not through a new action but through the **continuation of the action by which he gives being**, an action without movement or time. Likewise the continued influx of the sun conserves light in the air.\n\n## Article 2. Whether God immediately conserves every creature\n\n### Objections\n\n**1. [32767]** It seems that God conserves every creature immediately. God conserves things through the same action by which he creates them, as was said in Article 1, reply 4. But he immediately creates all things. Therefore he also immediately conserves all things.\n\n**2. [32768]** Further, each thing is closer to itself than to another. But no creature can be given the power to conserve itself. All the less, then, can a creature be given the power to conserve another. Therefore God conserves everything without any intermediate conserving cause.\n\n**3. [32769]** Further, an effect is conserved in being by what causes it not only in its coming-to-be but also in its being. Yet created causes, it appears, cause their effects only in their coming-to-be, since they act only by moving, as established above. They are therefore not conserving causes of their effects' being.\n\n### On the contrary — [32770]\n\nA thing is conserved through that by which it has being. But God gives being to things **through certain intermediate causes**. Therefore he also conserves things in being through intermediate causes.\n\n### I respond — [32771]\n\nAs explained, something conserves a thing in existence in two ways: first **indirectly and accidentally**, by removing or preventing a corrupting agent's action; second **directly and of itself**, because the existence of the other depends on it, as an effect's being depends on its cause. In both ways one finds a created thing conserving another.\n\nIt is plain that even among bodies many things prevent corrupting actions and are consequently called preservers: salt prevents meat from rotting, and many other examples could be given.\n\nIt is also found that the being of some effect depends upon a creature. Where there are many **ordered causes**, the effect must depend **first and principally on the First Cause**, and **secondarily on all the intermediate causes**. The First Cause, then, is principally responsible for the effect's conservation, and all intermediate causes secondarily so, the higher and nearer to the First Cause a cause is, the more its conservation extends.\n\nThis is why conservation and permanence are attributed to higher causes even among bodily things. Aristotle says in *Metaphysics* XII that the first motion—the daily motion—is the cause of the continuity of generation, whereas the second motion, along the zodiac, causes variation in generation and corruption. Astrologers likewise assign fixed and stable things to Saturn, the highest of the planets according to that scheme.\n\nWe must therefore say that **God conserves some things in being through intermediate causes**.\n\n### Replies\n\n**To objection 1 — [32772].** God immediately created all things, but in their creation he established an order whereby some things would depend on others, through which they would be conserved secondarily in being, always presupposing the principal conservation which belongs to God himself.\n\n**To objection 2 — [32773].** A proper cause conserves the effect depending upon it. Just as no effect can be given the ability to be the cause of itself, though it can be given the ability to cause another, so no effect can be given the ability to conserve itself, yet it can be given the power to conserve another.\n\n**To objection 3 — [32774].** No creature can be a cause of another's acquiring a **new form or disposition** except through some kind of change, since a creature always acts upon a presupposed subject. But once it has introduced that form or disposition into the effect, it can conserve it **without any further change in the effect**. When air first becomes illuminated, there is a change; but the conservation of that light involves no further change in the air, only the continued presence of the illuminating source.\n\n**End of Article 2.** Article 3 turns to the distinct problem of whether God can reduce something to nothingness, a point vital for divine freedom and the Avicennian necessity dispute, but not translated in this fascicle.\n\n## Latin-English control lemmata\n\n| Corpus ID | Latin | English significance |\n|---|---|---|\n| 32762 | *secundum fieri tantum, et non directe secundum esse eius* | The cause of coming-to-be is not necessarily the cause of continuous being |\n| 32762 | *causa huiusmodi formae secundum quod est in materia* | A natural same-species agent causes the form **as received in this matter**, not the abstract species form |\n| 32762 | *sic autem se habet omnis creatura ad Deum, sicut aer ad solem illuminantem* | Analogy of every creature's dependence on the First |\n| 32764 | *esse effectus dependet a causa essendi* | The effect's being depends on its cause of being |\n| 32766 | *non est per aliquam novam actionem* | Conservation is not an additional temporal action |\n| 32770 | *Deus dat esse rebus mediantibus aliquibus causis mediis* | The explicit existence of intermediate causes |\n| 32771 | *principaliter ... a causa prima; secundario vero ab omnibus causis mediis* | Conservation has a primary/secondary hierarchy |\n| 32772 | *praesupposita tamen principali conservatione* | Every intermediate preserving action presupposes God's principal conservation |\n| 32774 | *absque alia immutatione effectus ... conservat* | Conservation by secondary causes need not entail further change |\n\n## Historical and philosophical apparatus\n\n1. **The decisive correction:** Aquinas expressly grants intermediate conserving causes in the *sed contra*, response and replies of Article 2. The proposition “Aquinas denies all vertical intermediary causes while Avicenna affirms them” is demonstrably false. The real question is whether they assign the **same causal function** to the same kind of intermediate causes.\n2. **Similarity and difference in the builder and heat examples:** Avicenna *Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1–2 and Aquinas q.104 a.1 both deny the builder's continued action is necessary for a house's being and both make the causation of *fieri* different from *esse*. Aquinas, however, explicitly distinguishes the natural producer's causing the acquisition of a **form in this matter**, whereas Ibn Sīnā IX.5 assigns the newly actual substantial form's emanation to the separate Giver of Forms.\n3. **The “higher agent” in Aquinas a.1:** Aquinas uses the obsolete Aristotelian causal role of celestial bodies as an example of a cause giving a form according to its intelligible account; this is a piece of thirteenth-century natural philosophy, not a modern astronomical claim. Importantly, its **logical role** is to show a derivative being-giving cause under the First, not an autonomous creator.\n4. **Genuine difference from emanation:** q.104 a.1–2 does not alone establish Aquinas' doctrine of free creation. That belongs in the adjacent theological texts, especially q.104 a.3 on the divine ability to cease giving being and I q.19 on divine willing.\n5. **Identity versus participation:** “God alone is a being through his essence, because his essence is his existence” is the Thomist *ipsum esse subsistens* claim. Avicennian necessity-through-self is close, yet should not be identified with Aquinas' doctrine without separate analysis of real essence–esse composition and divine simplicity.\n6. **Do not conflate “immediate creation” and “intermediate conservation.”** Aquinas asserts both: God creates immediately, yet establishes a causal order whose intermediate causes conserve some effects subordinately. Any later claim that angels or celestial bodies can *create* ex nihilo would need a separate argument.\n7. **Textual status:** all Latin arguments, bodies and replies of both articles were read from the complete source and freshly translated; the full Latin remains reachable from the Corpus Thomisticum link. No new Latin recension or variant apparatus was attempted.\n\n## Links to Arabic primary translations\n\n- [Avicenna *Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1 — causes and conservation](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_E_AVICENNA_VI1.md)\n- [Avicenna *Ilāhiyyāt* VI.2 — causal hierarchy](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md)\n- [Avicenna *Ilāhiyyāt* IX.5 — natural causes and form-bestowal](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_D_AVICENNA_IX5.md)\n- [Lāhījī *Shawāriq* I:90–93 — sufficient causation](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_C_LAHIJI_PREFERENCE.md)\n- [Existing chapter 10 — conservation and secondary causes](chapters/10-conservation-first-cause-and-secondary-agency.md)\n\n**Completion stamp:** complete working English translation of both Latin scholastic units, paragraphs **32757–32774**; a new English reading of public-domain medieval Latin, with source anchors but no original Latin manuscript collation.\n","sourceUrl":"https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html","sourceUrls":["https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html"],"sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_F_AQUINAS_ST104.md","sourceCoverage":"Latin anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"aq-104b","file":"PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_G_AQUINAS_ST104_3_4.md","author":"Thomas Aquinas","work":"Summa theologiae","title":"Freedom and the possibility of annihilation","locus":"I q.104 aa.3–4","lang":"la","family":"Thomist comparison","kind":"primary","coverage":"two complete articles · working English","topics":["divine-will","conservation","creation"],"source":"https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html","md":"# Primary translation fascicle G — Aquinas, *Summa theologiae* I q.104, aa.3–4\n\n**GNAMMA new working English translation · 31 October 2026**\n\n**Unit:** the **complete two remaining articles** of Question 104 on the possibility and actuality of annihilation, with all objections, *sed contra*, central reply and replies to objections. Together with [Fascicle F](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_F_AQUINAS_ST104.md), this completes a continuous working English translation of all four articles of *ST* I q.104 (though not a new Latin textual recension).\n\n**Latin base:** [Corpus Thomisticum, Leonine text](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html), I q.104 a.3–4, paragraph IDs **[32775]–[32790]**, edited by Busa and Alarcón. The original Latin and the printing's scholarly metadata remain at the source link. The translation below is new, and not copied from existing published English translations.\n\n## Article 3 — Whether God can reduce something to nothingness\n\n### Objections\n\n**1. [32775]** It seems that God cannot reduce anything to nothingness. For Augustine says in the *Eighty-Three Questions* that God is not the cause of a thing's tending toward nonexistence. But that would be the case if God brought a creature back to nothing. Therefore God cannot bring anything to nothingness.\n\n**2. [32776]** Further, God is the cause of the existence of things through his goodness. As Augustine says in *On Christian Teaching*, we exist insofar as God is good. But God cannot fail to be good. Therefore he cannot bring it about that things fail to exist—which he would do if he reduced them to nothingness.\n\n**3. [32777]** Further, if God were to reduce a thing to nothingness, this would have to occur through some action. But this is impossible, since every action terminates in some being. Even a corrupting agent's action terminates in something generated, since the generation of one thing is the corruption of another. Thus God cannot reduce anything to nothingness.\n\n### On the contrary — [32778]\n\nScripture says in Jeremiah 10: “Correct me, Lord, yet with judgment and not in your anger, lest you bring me to nothing.”\n\n### I respond — [32779]\n\nSome have maintained that God produced things in being by acting from **necessity of nature**. If that were correct, God would be unable to reduce anything to nothingness, just as he cannot be changed from his own nature. But, as already established, this position is false and altogether contrary to the Catholic faith, which confesses that God produced things in being through his **free will**. Thus the Psalm says that the Lord did whatever he willed.\n\nThat God communicates existence to a creature therefore depends upon his will. Nor does God conserve things in existence otherwise than by **continuously bestowing existence upon them**, as previously explained. Consequently, just as before things existed he was able not to communicate existence to them and thus not to make them, so after things have been made he can withhold this influx of being from them, and thus they would cease to be. That is what it means for God to reduce them to nothingness.\n\n### Replies\n\n**To objection 1 — [32780].** Nonbeing has no essential cause, since nothing can be a cause except insofar as it is a being, and being, speaking essentially, is the cause of being. God therefore cannot be the cause of a thing's tending toward nonbeing. Rather, the creature has that from itself, insofar as it is **from nothing**. But God can be the cause of things' being reduced to nothingness **accidentally**, by withdrawing his action from them.\n\n**To objection 2 — [32781].** God's goodness is the cause of things, but not by necessity of nature, since divine goodness does not depend on created things; it causes them **through free will**. Thus, just as God was able not to produce things without any diminution of his goodness, so he can fail to conserve things in being without any injury to that goodness.\n\n**To objection 3 — [32782].** If God reduced a thing to nothingness, this would not be through an action, but through his **ceasing to act**.\n\n## Article 4 — Whether anything is actually reduced to nothingness\n\n### Objections\n\n**1. [32783]** It seems that something is reduced to nothingness. An end corresponds to a beginning. But at the beginning there was nothing except God. Therefore things will be led back to that end, so that nothing will remain except God; creatures will thus be annihilated.\n\n**2. [32784]** Further, every creature has a finite power. But no finite power extends to infinity: accordingly Aristotle argues in *Physics* VIII that a finite power cannot move for infinite time. No creature, then, can endure forever. Consequently, at some time it will be reduced to nothingness.\n\n**3. [32785]** Further, forms and accidents have no matter as one of their parts. But they sometimes cease to exist. It seems, then, that they are reduced to nothingness.\n\n### On the contrary — [32786]\n\nEcclesiastes 3 says: “I have learned that all the works which God has made endure forever.”\n\n### I respond — [32787]\n\nAmong the things God does with regard to creatures, some occur in accordance with the **natural course** of things; others he brings about miraculously, outside the order instilled in creatures, as will be explained below. What God will do according to the natural order placed in things can be considered from their own natures. Miraculous actions, meanwhile, are ordered to the manifestation of grace, as the Apostle says in 1 Corinthians 12, “To each is given the manifestation of the Spirit for some benefit,” before proceeding to speak of the working of miracles.\n\nThe natures of creatures, however, show that none is reduced to nothingness. For creatures are either **immaterial**, so that in them there is no potentiality for nonbeing, or **material**, so that at least matter always remains, since matter is incorruptible as the underlying subject of generation and corruption.\n\nNor does reducing something to nothingness belong to the manifestation of grace, since divine power and goodness are shown more clearly through the **conservation** of things in being. We must therefore say simply that **nothing at all is reduced to nothingness**.\n\n### Replies\n\n**To objection 1 — [32788].** That things were brought into existence after not having existed manifests the power of the producer. But their being reduced to nothingness would impede this manifestation, since God's power is chiefly shown by conserving things in being. Thus the Apostle says in Hebrews 1 that God sustains all things by the word of his power.\n\n**To objection 2 — [32789].** The creature's power for existing is only **receptive**, whereas the active power belongs to God, from whom the influx of being proceeds. Therefore the infinite endurance of things follows the **infinity of divine power**. Nevertheless some things have an appointed ability to persist only for a definite time, insofar as an opposing agent can prevent their reception of the influx of being; their finite power cannot resist that contrary agent indefinitely but only for a limited period. Therefore creatures without contraries, despite possessing finite power, endure forever.\n\n**To objection 3 — [32790].** Forms and accidents are not complete beings, because they do not subsist; rather, each is a **constituent aspect of a being**, being called a being insofar as something is through it. Even though they cease, they are not altogether reduced to nothingness in the mode of existence proper to them. This is not because a part of them remains, but because they remain in the **potency of matter or of the subject**.\n\n**End of *Summa theologiae* I, Question 104.** The next question (105) concerns God's action upon creatures, including the ability to move matter immediately to form and God's operation in every agent.\n\n## Latin anchors and cross-linguistic apparatus\n\n| Corpus ID | Latin expression | Translational / philosophical significance |\n|---|---|---|\n| 32779 | *agendo de necessitate naturae* | Acting from necessity of nature, rejected as a sufficient account of God's production |\n| 32779 | *res libera voluntate produxisse in esse* | Producing creatures through free will |\n| 32779 | *continue influit esse* | Conservation through a continuous bestowal of being |\n| 32780 | *non esse non habet causam per se* | Nonbeing has no positive cause *per se* |\n| 32780 | *per accidens ... subtrahendo scilicet suam actionem* | Annihilation, if it occurred, would arise from the **withdrawal** of the conserving act, not positive production of nothingness |\n| 32787 | *nec ulla earum in nihilum redigatur* | None of the ordinary creations undergo annihilation in the natural order |\n| 32789 | *potentia creaturae ad essendum est receptiva tantum* | Creaturely capacity to receive existence is distinct from the First Cause's power to give it |\n| 32790 | *remanent in potentia materiae vel subiecti* | Passing forms/accidents do not revert to nothingness but remain potentially in matter or subject |\n\n## Interpretive adjudication: Aquinas and Avicenna\n\n1. **Continuing dependence and free will are logically distinct.** *ST* I q.104 aa.1–2 gives the demonstration that being depends continuously upon a cause and permits the activity of intermediate conserving causes. Article 3 adds the explicitly further thesis that God *could have refrained* from creation or conservation because his effect depends on **free will**, not necessitation by nature. Avicenna's VI.1–2 argues for continual dependence, but it cannot therefore be identified without further premises with Aquinas' free act of creation.\n2. **Possibility versus actuality of annihilation.** Aquinas affirms the **possibility** that God could withhold being (a.3), while denying that any creature actually is or will be reduced to nothingness (a.4). Mixing these two would create a contradiction absent from his text.\n3. **No positive causal process produces nonbeing.** Both Avicenna VI.1's distinction between making existence and the incidental feature “after nonexistence,” and Aquinas a.3's account of annihilation by withdrawal, recognize a meaningful distinction between an act of bestowing being and descriptions of nonbeing.\n4. **Scope of historical natural philosophy.** The natural incorruptibility of celestial bodies in q.104 a.4 is a historical Aristotelian model, not an astronomical claim we adopt. The philosophical contrast between receptive finite capacity and unlimited primary power must be evaluated separately.\n5. **Forms and accidents.** Aquinas distinguishes the loss of a form from the complete annihilation of a *subsistent being*. This complicates readings of prime matter, substantial change, and the unity of a generated composite in the corresponding Avicennian and Lāhījī passages.\n6. **Primary documents.** [Earlier Aquinas articles 1–2](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_F_AQUINAS_ST104.md), [Avicenna VI.1](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_E_AVICENNA_VI1.md), [Avicenna VI.2](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md), [Avicenna IX.5](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_D_AVICENNA_IX5.md), [Lāhījī I:90–93](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_C_LAHIJI_PREFERENCE.md). Each retains a bounded coverage stamp.\n\n**Completion stamp:** complete working English translation of all of *ST* I q.104 **aa.3–4**, all objections and replies, paragraph IDs [32775]–[32790]. Together with Fascicle F, the entire four-article Question 104 is now in English across two fascicles. No claim of a new Latin recension or a complete translation of all Aquinas' work.\n","sourceUrl":"https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html","sourceUrls":["https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html"],"sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_G_AQUINAS_ST104_3_4.md","sourceCoverage":"Latin anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"lah-eman","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_04_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_EMANATION_UNITY.md","author":"ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī","work":"Gawhar-i murād","title":"The Rule of the One and first emanation","locus":"II.3.4 opening pp.290–293","lang":"fa","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"bounded opening argument","source":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/290?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/290?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/291?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/292?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/293?lang=ar"],"topics":["cosmology","existence","causation","unity"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 04 — Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, II.3.4, opening argument: One, first intellect, and intermediaries\n\n**31 October 2026 · Persian → working English, pp.290–293**\n\n**Work**: ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, Article II, Part 3, Chapter 4, “The first emanated being and the order of existents.” **Textual boundary**: **the opening pp.290–293**, not the entirety of Chapter 4 (whose later discussion of transmitted sayings continues thereafter). Digital Persian print: [290](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/290?lang=ar), [291](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/291?lang=ar), [292](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/292?lang=ar), [293](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/293?lang=ar), Nashr-i Sāya, Tehran.\n\n## p.290 — why only one immediate effect proceeds from the true One\n\n> از واحد حقيقى ... صادر نتواند شد بالذّات ... مگر واحدى\n\nLāhījī reports the philosophers' position: from the true One, which contains no plurality either real or conceptual, **only one effect can proceed directly at a single level**, insofar as it is a true One. To produce a determinate effect rather than any other possible effect, the cause would need some distinguishing respect. In the absolutely simple One, such a distinction cannot be an intrinsic part or added property, for that would import plurality. It must be identical to the essence. One and the same respect identical to the essence cannot directly explain two genuinely different effects; if it could, the two effects would fail to differ, or that single respect would be composite. The only coherent alternative is that one effect follows the cause essentially and the other by the mediation of an accidental or derivative respect. Since the Necessary Existent is absolutely one, the first immediate effect must itself be one.\n\n**Reported variant:** p.290 n.2 B/C `شركت` in place of `مركّب`; the resulting sentence should be checked in the Persian print image before choosing a definitive edition. We translate the main text's “composite.”\n\n## p.291 — the first effect must be an intellect\n\nThe first effect must have a simple extra-mental quiddity, and neither its existence nor its constitution may depend on some still earlier effect: otherwise that earlier effect would be the first. Only an **intellectual substance** meets the conditions. Accidents require substances; body is constituted of matter and form; form needs matter for individuation, matter needs form for subsistence; and soul requires bodily preparedness either inherent (as in the celestial case) or newly acquired (as in human souls). None is first except a separate intellect. Once the first intellect proceeds, it contains derivative plurality, not as several immediate first effects but through its own concomitants. Its particular identity or existence is first; it has quiddity, intrinsic possibility relative to that quiddity, necessity through the first cause, cognition of itself, and cognition of its first cause. Hence six considerations arise: existence and quiddity (the same externally, distinct in thought); possibility and self-knowledge relative to itself; necessity through another and knowledge of its cause relative to its cause.\n\n**Variant note:** p.291 n.1 B reads `استبعادى فطرى` for `استعداد ذاتى فطرى`; n.2 C `بالعروض` for `بالعرض`, n.3 B `مخصوصه` for the longer form. The nature of the celestial soul's “preparedness” merits review.\n\n## p.292 — multiple effects through nonindependent respects\n\nThe respects belonging to the first intellect by relation to its first cause are nobler; through such respects proceeds the second intellect, and through the lesser respects the first sphere. The sphere itself has a nobler soul/form and a lower bodily matter. These respects are **not separately existing external things**, but they have a foundation in *nafs al-amr*: explanatory considerations differ in ways relevant to causation. Nor do such concomitants require a distinct act of making; their production is derivative upon the production of what has them. Hence nothing like immediate plurality follows from the absolutely One. The same reasoning proceeds from the second intellect to the second sphere and third intellect, and so on until the ninth gives rise to the tenth. The tenth intellect, with celestial cooperation, provides the material basis for sublunary things capable of acquiring various successive dispositions.\n\n**Variant note:** B omits `افتاده مر`; A/C give plural `توانند` instead of singular `تواند`, affecting agreement but not the theory that dependent respects need no independent making.\n\n## p.293 — affirming the single divine existential agent despite intermediaries\n\nThe tenth intellect supplies, according to differing dispositions, the elemental forms of simple and composite generated things. But from the proposition that multiple effects require multiple causal respects, it does **not follow conversely** that every cause with multiple respects must produce equally many effects. Universal affirmative statements do not simply reverse; moreover the intellects do not all possess the same specific reality. There is therefore no entailment to infinitely many intellects and spheres.\n\nThe Rule of the One and the hierarchy of procession, Lāhījī says, need not contradict religion or divine will. Arbitrariness and the preponderance of an alternative without a determinant are impossible; the will's attachment to a specified alternative also requires a specifying respect. Nor does affirming intermediaries compromise the unity of divine agency. **The intermediate cause does not independently give existence or produce the effect as its true creative agent**: it mediates the arrival of the effusion of existence from the remote cause to the effect. Thus the critical philosophers who affirm necessary intermediaries may nevertheless say: “There is no agent conferring existence except God” (*lā muʾaththira fī l-wujūd illā Allāh*). Lāhījī then connects the theory with the report “The first thing God created was intellect”; the transmitted-text interpretation proceeds beyond the portion translated here.\n\n**Source note:** p.293 includes a reference to *Biḥār al-anwār* I:97 for the reported hadith; this is a religious gloss in Lāhījī's own discussion and is not an independently validated prophetic/Imāmī isnād in this edition.\n\n## Critical interpretive results\n\n- **Important correction to the initial description “Active Intellect is a giver of existence”:** p.293 explicitly distinguishes an **intermediate conduit of the effusion of existence** from the **one true existential agent**. This is not a denial that the tenth intellect bestows forms at its level. Compare [Chapter 6](LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md); separate higher existential agency, separate intelligible mediation and material disposition.\n- **Importance for *nafs al-amr*:** explanatory causal respects are not independent external existents but are nonetheless *nafs al-amrī*, i.e. not arbitrary inventions of the mind. This is a direct Persian witness to later Avicennian use of a real-founded, not simply independently subsistent, explanatory order.\n- **Difference from Aquinas remains real:** both can affirm that contingent existence is sourced from the First, while disagreeing about necessary procession and divine freedom. Compare *ST* I q.104 aa.3–4.\n- **Critical limit:** This is a translation of pp.290–293, a coherent *opening argument*, not the rest of Chapter 4, and does not independently verify the printed variant witnesses.\n","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/290?lang=ar","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_04_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_EMANATION_UNITY.md","sourceCoverage":"Persian anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"lah-eman-cont","title":"The fixed-star objection and limits of the ten-intellect count","author":"ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī","work":"Gawhar-i murād","locus":"Article II, Book III, Section IV pp.294–297","lang":"fa","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"previously omitted continuation · page-ordered working English","topics":["cosmology","unity","intellect","causation"],"sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/294?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/294?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/295?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/296?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/297?lang=ar"],"sourceGrade":"contextual-rendering-checked-pages","md":"# Context-recovery fascicle 12 — Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, II.3.4, conclusion and objections (pp. 294–297)\n\n**New working English translation of the previously omitted continuation · 31 October 2026**\n\n**Precise location:** ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, **Article II (مقاله دوم), Chapter/Book III (باب سوم), Section/Faṣl IV (فصل چهارم)**, “On the First Emanated Being and an Indication of the Arrangement of Existents,” **continuation pp. 294–297** (Nāshr-i Sāya printed pagination). [p.294](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/294?lang=ar) · [p.295](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/295?lang=ar) · [p.296](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/296?lang=ar) · [p.297](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/297?lang=ar). The **beginning and initial demonstration of the chapter, pp. 290–293, have already been rendered separately**: [preceding fascicle](LATE_FASCICLE_04_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_EMANATION_UNITY.md). Together these locate the complete printed chapter before Section V starts on p.298. This file does not independently collate any manuscript.\n\n## 1. What the author is answering\n\n**Earlier lemma (pp. 290–293):** From the absolutely One no more than one effect proceeds directly in the same respect. The first effect is an intellect; plural **respects of this first effect** permit mediated diversity without an extra simultaneous effect coming directly from the First. Matter, form and the sublunary world occur at later stages. Lāhījī distinguishes a **mediate causal channel** from the one true giver of existence.\n\n**Problem carried into pp. 294–297:** Is the proposed procession identifiable with Islamic descriptions of the first creation? Must the chain contain precisely ten separate intellects? Can the limited respects in the early intellects explain the immense numerical multiplicity of fixed stars? The last is an objection attributed to **Shaykh al-Ishrāq (Suhrawardī)**. The conclusion on p.297 is a reply to that objection, *not* a freestanding assertion about the number of stars.\n\n### [p. 294] The First Intellect as intellect, spirit and pen\n\nIn certain traditions the first created reality is named “my intellect,” and in others “my spirit”; other reports describe the Pen as the first of created things. Lāhījī argues that these varying expressions need not contradict one another if they all allude to the First Intellect. He also recalls his earlier account of the Prophet's existential rank as related, in the returning series of being, to the First Intellect in the descending series. The identification is explicitly an **interpretation of reported traditions**, not a further physical demonstration.\n\nThe same principle supplies an analogy for the Pen: the First Intellect mediates the effusion of knowledges and perfections into lower receptivities as a pen impresses characters on a tablet. An external receptive field and the plane of *nafs al-amr* can, in that analogy, be called the clear book, while levels of simple and composite existents are the script inscribed through intelligible mediation. The Divine Power is the ultimate agency: a suitable metaphorical “pen” for it is a separate intellect. Lāhījī also mentions an interpretation of Qurʾān 68:1 involving intelligences or immaterial angels and introduces Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī's explanation from the *Tafsīr al-kabīr*. **These reports belong to the exegetical subsection and must not be treated as identical to the earlier demonstrative premise.**\n\n*Original-language control:* «و نيز چون عقل اوّل واسطهء فيضان علوم و كمالات است». [Source p.294](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/294?lang=ar).\n\n### [p. 295] The number ten is an explanatory lower bound, not an impossibility proof against eleven\n\nContinuing the report attributed to Rāzī, Lāhījī notes traditions identifying the first creature as an intellect, a pen, or a certain simple substance, and construes their subject as a single underlying referent. He then makes a qualification indispensable to an accurate account of later Avicennism: **the philosophers do not profess to have demonstrated that more than ten separate intellects are impossible**. The standard count is advanced because the arguments discussed have reached that number.\n\nAccordingly there may be additional vertically ordered intellects between the Necessary Existent and the intellect responsible for the first celestial sphere. Avicenna's *Ishārāt* speaks **indefinitely** of “an intellectual substance” from which another intellectual substance and a heavenly body follow, not of a uniquely demonstrated numerical first intellect directly producing a uniquely demonstrated first sphere. Lāhījī then reports **Ṭūsī's commentary** as explicitly treating that formulation as noncommittal on whether the very first intellect is the direct principle of the first sphere: there is no demonstration fixing that identity.\n\nOn an older cosmological identification of the first sphere with the sphere of the fixed stars, its actual diversity might exceed the explanatory aspects of the absolutely first intellect. The cause of that sphere might therefore be a **later** intellect. Lāhījī attributes this caution to Ṭūsī and draws the consequence that the second intellect could potentially be the source of the fixed stars.\n\n*Original-language control:* «قول به تعيين عدد عشره در عقول مجرّده ، مجزوم به حكما نيست». [Source p.295](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/295?lang=ar).\n\n### [p. 296] Reply to the fixed-star plurality objection: twelve possible effects\n\nLāhījī now develops the response to the objection that the early intellects lack enough distinct aspects to explain the fixed stars. Take the absolutely One as **A** and its sole immediate effect as **B**. At the second causal level we can have **C** proceeding from A through B, as well as **D** from B itself. Thus already two effects can coexist at the second level without violating the rule that only one immediate effect proceeds from A. If we also allow the lower principle B to operate in a more complicated mediation involving the higher source, the range of possibilities increases.\n\nRetaining only C and D at the second level, twelve effects are distinguishable at the third level through different ordered or combined channels: **six** grounded in A by means of C, D, C+D, B+C, B+D, and B+C+D; **three** grounded in B through C, D, or C+D; and **three** proceeding from C alone, D alone, or C and D together. This enumeration explains why multiple derivative respects and mediating configurations may yield far more effects than the very small number of immediately produced separate intellects would suggest.\n\nIf further orders of mediation—such as the operation of a lower source through a higher source—are admitted, the range is greater still. Thus the numerical objection cannot simply infer that the first intellect needs one intrinsically distinct aspect for every observed star. **This is a combinatorial possibility argument, not a newly verified celestial count or a proof that all listed channels actually occur.**\n\n*Original-language control:* «در مرتبهء سوّم دوازده معلول متحقق تواند شد». [Source p.296](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/296?lang=ar).\n\n### [p. 297] Conclusion: the objection of Shaykh al-Ishrāq\n\nEven the simplified third-level enumeration can be augmented by taking into account additional causal respects in the first intellect and differing orderings of intermediaries. On those suppositions the numerical possibilities become too numerous to list exhaustively. Lāhījī therefore concludes that the criticism attributed to **Shaykh al-Ishrāq**, that the second intellect has insufficient plurality to account for the many fixed stars, is answered.\n\nThis is the **end of Section IV**. The following heading on p.298 introduces Section V concerning **elemental forms and mixtures**. The two sections are logically connected: IV answers how mediated multiplicity is possible; V addresses how sublunary matter takes different forms. Neither is an independent assertion about substantial generation.\n\n*Original-language control:* «پس مندفع شد تشنيع شيخ اشراق بر مشّائين». [Source p.297](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/297?lang=ar).\n\n## 2. Textual and attribution apparatus\n\n- **Boundaries:** Section IV is pp.290–297. This continuation renders pp.294–297; the existing prior fascicle renders pp.290–293. A split file must not be misrepresented as a separate fourth/fifth chapter.\n- **Voices:** Lāhījī is the narrator; *reported ḥadīth* and a passage attributed to **Rāzī's *Tafsīr*** provide interpretive correspondence; **Ṭūsī** supplies the quoted reading of Avicenna's *Ishārāt*; **Suhrawardī** is the opponent whose plurality objection is answered. The report of any earlier author here is **not** an independent collation of that author's own book.\n- **Printed textual differences:** p.294 marks omissions of «عقل» and «صيغه» in selected lettered witnesses; p.295 reports «اعظم» for «اول» in the sphere discussion; p.296 records «معلول» instead of a phrase identifying a second effect; p.297 reports «ترتب» for «ترتيب». These are **editor-reported readings**, not variants established by this project.\n- **Critical logical distinction:** “Not demonstrated that ten must be the maximum” is **not** the thesis that the series is actually infinite. The twelve-effect illustration demonstrates explanatory *possibility* rather than an observed count.\n- **Source crosswalk:** [prior Section IV pp.290–293](LATE_FASCICLE_04_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_EMANATION_UNITY.md) → [Section V pp.298–300](LATE_CONTEXT_FASCICLE_13_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_CH5_ELEMENTS.md) → [Section VI pp.301–303](LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md).\n\n**Edition state:** connected contextual **working English rendering** of an entire four-page continuation, with checked printed headings, speaker layers, page-located source links and summary of the stated objections/replies. **Not a sentence-aligned Persian critical edition**, nor a textual edition of Rāzī, Ṭūsī or Suhrawardī themselves.\n","sourceCoverage":"Persian anchors only; whole printed page at linked source","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_CONTEXT_FASCICLE_12_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_CH4_COMPLETION.md","manuscriptCollated":false,"status":"Working context-rich English with source page locators; no independent Persian manuscript collation"},{"id":"lah-element","title":"Elements, mixtures and the objection to friction-only generation","author":"ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī","work":"Gawhar-i murād","locus":"Article II, Book III, Section V pp.298–300","lang":"fa","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"previously omitted complete section · working English","topics":["forms","natural-generation","matter","cosmology"],"sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/298?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/298?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/299?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/300?lang=ar"],"sourceGrade":"contextual-rendering-checked-pages","md":"# Context-recovery fascicle 13 — Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, II.3.5, elemental forms and mixture (pp.298–300)\n\n**New contextual working English translation · 31 October 2026**\n\n**Exact location:** ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, **Article II (مقاله دوم), Book/Chapter III (باب سوم), Section V (فصل پنجم)**, “On the causes of the difference of the forms of the four elements, and the causes of mixtures that are the principles of the composition of composites,” pp.298–300, Nāshr-i Sāya printed text. [Page 298](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/298?lang=ar) · [page 299](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/299?lang=ar) · [page 300](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/300?lang=ar).\n\n**Editorial significance:** This entire section was missing between the earlier translations of Section IV (procession of separate intellects) and Section VI (rational souls and forms from the tenth intellect). That omission obscured **what claim Lāhījī was answering**, and made the later form-bestowal thesis seem detached from the natural-philosophical problem of material preparation. The restored sequence is [IV (pp.290–297)](LATE_CONTEXT_FASCICLE_12_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_CH4_COMPLETION.md) → **V (pp.298–300)** → [VI (pp.301–303)](LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md) → [VII (pp.304–306)](LATE_FASCICLE_02_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_TEMPORAL_CAUSATION.md) → [VIII (pp.307–309)](LATE_FASCICLE_03_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_DIVINE_AGENCY.md).\n\n## I. The question and the reported rival explanation — p.298\n\n**Question:** If the lower world shares a common material basis, why do the four elements possess *different* substantial forms? By what processes do the elements combine into mixed bodies disposed for the forms of more complex substances?\n\n**Rival account, explicitly reported from Ibn Sīnā's *Shifāʾ*, not endorsed by Avicenna:** Certain people professing natural philosophy maintained that circular motion of the celestial sphere brings it into frictional contact with an initially stationary body inside it. The contact heats the outer part of that body until it becomes **fire**. The remote central part remains relatively motionless, becomes cold and dense, and becomes **earth**. Between them, the part closer to the fire is less intensely hot and moist, yielding **air**, while that closer to earth is colder and moist, yielding **water**. Thus differences in heat, density, distance and contact allegedly explain the substantial coming-to-be of the four elements.\n\nThis account, if read without the introductory attribution and subsequent criticism, could be falsely represented as **Lāhījī's** or **Avicenna's adopted theory**. It is instead **the view targeted by the ensuing objection**.\n\n*Original text anchor:* «شيخ ابو على ابن سينا نقل كرده در شفا از جماعتى منتسبين به علم حكمت». [Printed p.298](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/298?lang=ar).\n\n## II. Avicenna's hylomorphic objection and the separate form-bestower — p.299\n\n**Reported Avicennian reply:** After relating the friction-and-cooling hypothesis, Avicenna judges it unsound. The hypothesis tacitly requires that a body first exist with no **specific substantial form**, and only later acquire fire, earth, air or water through movement and rest. Such a wholly indeterminate body, possessing merely generic corporeality but no specific form, is impossible. Corporeality in the abstract is like a **genus**; specific elemental forms stand to it as determining **differentiae**. Just as an actual genus does not subsist apart from any differentia, matter cannot remain as an actual body without any such constitution.\n\n**Positive alternative attributed to Avicenna and endorsed in Lāhījī's exposition:** Both the material basis of the elements and their differing substantial forms proceed through the **tenth intellect**, with cooperative causal roles assigned to the celestial spheres. That cooperation may involve **four spheres**, groups of celestial spheres or one sphere under four distinct relations. The rival explanation of heat and cold may still describe **why bodies occupy or are conserved in particular places**; it does *not* explain the original conferral of their specific forms.\n\n**Further citation to *al-Mabdaʾ wa-l-maʿād*:** As elemental bodies undergo generation and corruption, their proximate principles must include something receptive of alteration. An intellect by itself cannot be their sole proximate natural principle. The shared matter is correlated with a common celestial character, identified in the historical cosmology with circular motion, while the diversity of elemental forms requires diversified causes and motions. This **real causal role of physical change** must not be deleted when discussing the higher source of form.\n\n*Original text anchors:* «و بعد از نقل اين كلام حكم كرده به عدم صحّت اين كلام»; «صور عناصر نيز فايض شود ... از عقل عاشر به معاونت افلاك». [Printed p.299](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/299?lang=ar).\n\n## III. Common matter, material actualisation, and mixtures — p.300\n\nThe different directions and rates of celestial motions, as understood in this historical account, differentiate the relevant causal preparations. **Matter cannot subsist without some form**, so its constitution cannot depend only on the common nature of the celestial spheres. On the other hand, since the matter persists across the replacement of this or that elemental form, no *single particular* currently existing form can, by itself, be the sole permanent ground of matter. Thus its persistence is accounted for by **both** the higher common causal nature and some constitutive form; Lāhījī does not posit bare unformed matter as an actually existing intermediary.\n\nHe then classifies causes of **mixture** into two types:\n\n1. Differences in the relations between terrestrial elements and celestial influences. By his example, the sun's alignment with a region gives illumination, illumination gives heat, heat produces rarefaction or upward displacement, that displacement removes a body from its usual place, and bodies thereby mix.\n2. The natural powers and configurations that proceed, in his cosmology, from the heavens into the natures, forms and souls of bodies. These powers move their own matter and other matter—for example, nutritive powers—and consequently bring about mixtures and temperaments.\n\nDifferent quantities and qualities of the constituent elements produce **different temperaments**, hence diverse degrees and kinds of material preparedness. Finally, **different specific forms of compounds** are said to emanate from the Giver of Forms in correspondence with that differentiated preparedness. These final sentences provide the immediate premise of **Section VI**, where Lāhījī argues that the responsible separate source for the rational soul and all species is the tenth intellect.\n\n*Original text anchor:* «به حسب استعدادات مختلفه ، صور مركّبات متخالفه از واهب الصور فايض شود». [Printed p.300](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/300?lang=ar).\n\n## Argument sequence: who says what, and what replies to what?\n\n| Step | Historical voice and location | Claim or question | Function in the argument |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | An unnamed group **reported** in Ibn Sīnā, p.298 | Frictional heating and cooling generate elemental forms | **Target view**, not the adopted explanation |\n| 2 | **Ibn Sīnā's criticism as retold by Lāhījī**, p.299 | The target presupposes corporeal matter without any species form | Hylomorphic **objection** |\n| 3 | Ibn Sīnā, endorsed in the exposition, p.299 | Separate intellect bestows forms in cooperation with celestial conditions | **Positive replacement** |\n| 4 | Ibn Sīnā's *al-Mabdaʾ wa-l-maʿād* as **reported by Lāhījī**, pp.299–300 | Common nature explains shared matter; differentiated motions explain preparedness | **Further explanation** of material and formal diversity |\n| 5 | Lāhījī's synthesis, p.300 | Real local causal sequences lead to different mixtures and temperaments | **Mechanism of preparation**, not independent creative bestowal |\n| 6 | Lāhījī, final p.300 → Section VI p.301 | Different forms of composites emanate with differentiated material preparation | **Bridge** to the separate tenth intellect and soul |\n\n## Notes on the textual edition\n\n- The *Shifāʾ* reference printed in Lāhījī's edition is **Ilāhiyyāt IX.5** (the cited edition includes p.536 in its own pagination); the existing [continuous working English translation of IX.5](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_D_AVICENNA_IX5.md) gives the larger Avicennian context. **Do not regard these as one edition with a shared page numbering**.\n- The printed edition at p.298 records omission of the word «مركبات» in A/B/C and several minor differences about bodily movement and temperature; p.299 records «صور» for «صورت» (B/C), «تخصص» for «تخصيص» (C), and the absence of «مستديره» (C); p.300 records «نسيب» for «نسبت» (B/C). These are **reported editorial variants**, not independently inspected manuscripts.\n- **The argument's four levels are separate:** (i) intrinsic form constituting the composite; (ii) natural physical action and mixture; (iii) celestial influences in medieval cosmology; (iv) tenth-intellect mediated bestowal. These distinctions allow meaningful comparison with Aquinas while respecting the historical account.\n- **Exact completion boundary:** This document supplies a connected working English rendering of the **three-page complete Section V**, reconstructed from its page-ordered original Persian paragraphs. It does **not** constitute a bilingual line-by-line diplomatic edition or independent Persian manuscript stemma, nor does it independently collate the *Shifāʾ* and *Mabdaʾ wa-l-maʿād* citations.\n\n**Next passage in this same argument:** [Section VI, pp.301–303, why the tenth intellect bestows rational souls and specific forms](LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md).\n","sourceCoverage":"Persian anchors only; whole printed page at linked source","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_CONTEXT_FASCICLE_13_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_CH5_ELEMENTS.md","manuscriptCollated":false,"status":"Working context-rich English with source page locators; no independent Persian manuscript collation"},{"id":"lah-active","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md","author":"ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī","work":"Gawhar-i murād","title":"The tenth intellect and natural forms","locus":"II.3.6 pp.301–303","lang":"fa","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"bounded complete chapter","source":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/301?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/301?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/302?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/303?lang=ar"],"topics":["intellect","forms","natural-generation","individuation"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 01 — Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, II.3.6: the tenth intellect, souls and natural forms\n\n**31 October 2026 · Persian primary source → new working English translation**\n\n**Author**: ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī (d. 1661/2). **Work**: *Gawhar-i murād* (گوهر مراد), Article II, Book/Part 3, Chapter 6. **Witness**: searchable Persian edition, Nashr-i Sāya, Tehran, print pp. **301–303**, [p.301](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/301?lang=ar), [p.302](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/302?lang=ar), [p.303](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/303?lang=ar). The HTML includes abbreviated notes reporting readings of witnesses **الف، ب، ج**, but *their manuscript identities and stemma have not been independently verified*. The complete original Persian is at the linked pages; brief Persian anchors are printed below.\n\n## Heading, printed p.301\n\n> فصل ششم ... در بيان آنكه علّت فيضان نفوس ناطقه و ساير صور نوعيّهء مركبات ، عقل عاشر است\n\n**Translation:** “Chapter Six, on showing that the cause of the emanation of rational souls and of the other specific forms of composite things is the tenth intellect, also called the Active Intellect.”\n\n### p.301 — why a single species has multiple individuals\n\nRational souls, as already established, are many in number but agree in specific reality. On account of their number they cannot all emanate immediately, at one level, from the Necessary Existent, who is an absolutely true One. And on account of their agreement in species, they cannot emanate in a sequence in which some souls are causes of other souls, for that would introduce a gradation in an essential determination: if the quiddity is the same in all individuals, causal priority among them would introduce an essential priority that is not present in the species. The situation differs for intellects that are distinct in their realities. Rational souls likewise cannot emanate from a celestial body, for the soul is more noble than a body, and the nobler is not the effect of the baser. Nor can they emanate from the first effect: if its many respects differed in reality, what issued through each would differ in specific reality; if those respects were of one reality, they could not account for many individuals without matter.\n\n**Apparatus**: at p.301 n.1, `صدورشان` (“their procession”) has `صدورش` in B and C. This does not change the argument's contrast between one species and multiple rational souls.\n\n### p.302 — preparedness and individuating bodies\n\nMany individuals of a single species come about only through matter and material accidents, which are absent from the first effect. The same problem excludes the other separate intellects preceding the tenth. Although each of these intellects is associated with a sphere containing matter, celestial matter does not receive generation and corruption and therefore cannot be responsible for a numerical plurality within one species. The tenth intellect, by contrast, is the source of the matter of generated things. Thus a single respect in it can produce the species of rational soul while its individuals multiply through matters with different dispositions arising from different temperaments. The source of the rational soul must accordingly be an intellect also responsible for sublunary matter. The individuality of each soul is derived from matter made ready by a particular disposition—that is, the body. If the existence of an intellectual principle for each sphere is otherwise established, this principle will be the tenth intellect. Lāhījī asks whether the present argument itself can establish the entire number of intellects and says that, on close examination, it can, as Ibn Sīnā argued in *al-Mabdaʾ wa-l-maʿād*.\n\n**Variants**: p.302 n.1 B,C read `معلولات اوّل` for `معلول اوّل`; n.2 witness A omits `نيز`; n.3 A has `باشند`; n.4 C adds `كه`; n.5 B,C omit `امّا`.\n\n### p.303 — a hierarchy, not separate species-lords\n\nThe different dispositions of the matter of generated things, which account for the numerical multiplication of rational souls, arise with the assistance of celestial motions and mixtures. The corresponding intellect must therefore be placed at the level of the lunar sphere, the last of the spheres. It cannot be the first effect, since other spheres precede it; nor can it be the product of the sphere, since it is nobler than the sphere. It must proceed from a further intellect, and the same argument may be repeated until it reaches an intellect ordered with the first sphere and proceeding, along with that sphere, from the first intellect. The familiar ordered series of ten intellects follows.\n\nOnce the tenth intellect has been established as the source of rational souls, it is likewise the source of the different species of generated things, through its different causal respects and the different dispositions of receptive matter. **Specific diversity belongs to diverse causal respects; numerical diversity within a species belongs to diverse dispositions.** Since the rational soul is the noblest of generated species, it derives through the noblest respect; other composites proceed through other respects. What the Illuminationists call a “lord of the species” (*rabb al-nawʿ*)—the source that oversees and pours out each species' perfections—can thus be referred to the **tenth intellect** under its various respects. There is no need to posit additional horizontally arranged separate intellects, one for every natural species.\n\n**Variants**: p.303 notes 1–5 record B `براى` for `بنابر`, A `اوّل` for `معلول`, A `و` for `پس`, B omission of `انواع`, and B `به ترتيب` for `به تربيت`. The last variant affects whether “ordering” or “nurturing” is the stated relation, so the main translation retains the base's stronger nurturing formulation.\n\n## Editorial adjudication\n\n**What the text demonstrates:** a named late Safavid post-*Tajrīd* philosopher **explicitly retains** the separate tenth intellect as the bestower of human rational souls and **other natural specific forms**, and uses matter's differentiated *istiʿdādāt* to explain distinct individuals. He further *rejects the need* to multiply species-lords horizontally. This is a first-hand textual answer to the earlier open question whether Lāhījī had abandoned a separate Giver of Forms: **he had not, at least here**.\n\n**What it does not show:** that the rational soul is intrinsically material, that every late commentator agrees, that Lāhījī denies real natural agent efficacy, or that the ten-intellect astronomy is empirically credible. In particular, later [Chapter 7](LATE_FASCICLE_02_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_TEMPORAL_CAUSATION.md) distinguishes sustaining existence from time-specific material preparation.\n\n**Coverage**: the source's entire Chapter 6 is short and spans exactly printed pp.301–303; this translation follows its argument paragraph by paragraph, with selectively reported printed variants, **not manuscript autopsy**. The edition is a new English translation, not an independently established Persian critical text.\n","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/301?lang=ar","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md","sourceCoverage":"Persian anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"lah-temporal","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_02_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_TEMPORAL_CAUSATION.md","author":"ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī","work":"Gawhar-i murād","title":"How temporal things arise from eternal causes","locus":"II.3.7 pp.304–306","lang":"fa","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"bounded complete chapter","source":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/304?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/304?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/305?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/306?lang=ar"],"topics":["temporal","causation","conservation"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 02 — Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, II.3.7: how temporal events depend on an eternal cause\n\n**31 October 2026 · Persian primary source → new working English translation**\n\n**Work**: ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, Article II, Part 3, **Chapter 7**, “On the cause of the origination of events and the connection of temporal events with the eternal.” **Witness**: Nashr-i Sāya Persian text pp. **304–306**: [304](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/304?lang=ar), [305](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/305?lang=ar), [306](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/306?lang=ar). Printed notes record variant sigla A, B, C but no manuscript images have been collated here.\n\n## p.304 — two different grounds of causal dependence\n\n> سبب حاجت ممكن به علت ، امكان است ... نه حدوث\n\n**Translation:** “The cause of the contingent being's need for a cause is its contingency, not its temporal origination.”\n\nAs already established, the ground of a contingent being's need for a cause is possibility—this is the doctrine of most philosophers and the critical investigators among theologians—not temporal beginning, as another group of theologians maintains. “Cause” in this first claim means what **bestows existence** and makes existence prevail over nonexistence. In truth, the character of possibility is the ground of that dependence, whether the possible being begins in time or not. By a “temporally originated thing” here we mean something whose being is preceded by a time in which it did not exist, as in everyday events. By an “eternal” possible thing we mean something not so preceded, even if its being follows absolute nonbeing in an order other than time, as in the traditional example of the celestial sphere. This pair of terms must not be confused with the earlier discussion of origination and eternity of the world: what is “eternal” in the present temporal sense can nonetheless be originated in the earlier essential sense. All possible things are originated in that latter sense, but only some in the present temporal sense. Hence all possible things, temporal or not, need the being-giving cause equally. A temporal event has an **additional** need.\n\n## p.305 — *mubqiya* and *muḥditha*; why motion matters\n\nIts additional need is for a cause of origination (*ʿilla muḥditha*), meaning what accounts for the fact that the existence-bestowing cause favors its existence at this particular segment of time, rather than an earlier segment. Otherwise this time would be selected without a selector, just as without a cause bestowing existence, being rather than nonbeing would prevail without a determinant. A possible being with no preceding time needs no such additional cause.\n\nScholars therefore call what makes existence itself prevail the **conserving cause** (*ʿilla mubqiya*), since the contingent thing needs it throughout its continuance, and what determines the specific time the **originating cause** (*ʿilla muḥditha*), needed only for the event's temporal inception. The temporally determining cause must itself become operative at the event's time; otherwise the effect would fail to accompany its complete cause. Repeating the question for every new cause threatens an infinite regress of newly arisen causes. There must therefore be something that is new in one respect yet stable in another: **motion**, which is stable with respect to its own nature but constantly renewed in relation to the boundaries of its course. Here Lāhījī's model presupposes a motion without a temporal beginning in this specific sense.\n\n**Printed variants**: B omits `چون` at p.305 n.1, B,C omit `گويند` n.2, A has `نماند` versus `باقى نمانده` n.3. These do not alter the explicit distinction between conserving and time-specifying causes.\n\n## p.306 — the gradual acquisition of preparedness\n\nIn this cosmological account the required continuous motion is celestial motion. It is by that motion that a temporally originated thing can be the effect of the eternal Necessary Existent without the effect being delayed after a completed sufficient cause; thus temporal things are connected with the eternal. Celestial movement specifies the time of an event when the event has a **matter or underlying subject** whose preparedness for it grows gradually through celestial movements and configurations. When that matter or subject has become fully ready, the event's existence necessarily flows to it from the **giver of existence**. Complete preparedness is thus the proximate cause determining the *time* of its occurrence, while celestial motions and configurations are remoter causes. Accordingly, a temporal event is preceded both by motions/configurations and by matter or subject. This explains the philosophers' maxim that every originated thing is preceded by **matter and duration**. “Duration” means time measured by celestial motion; “matter” is used broadly, embracing both prime matter (*hayūlā*) and an underlying subject of accidents.\n\n**Printed variants**: A omits `به` (p.306 n.1), reads `حادثى` for `حادث` (n.2), `استعدادى` for `استعداد` (n.3); B `موضوعى` for `موضوع` (n.4), `تخصيص` for `تخصّص` (n.5), and omits `از مادّه به معنى` (n.6). The n.5 change makes the proximate cause's act of **selecting** the event's time more explicit; either reading preserves the distinction.\n\n## Philosophical apparatus\n\n1. **Three causal tasks must be distinguished:** (i) giving a possible thing existence, (ii) conserving it while it exists, and (iii) specifying the exact moment when a *temporally beginning* event comes to be. Lāhījī's p.305 *mubqiya/muḥditha* pair refers to (i)/(ii) versus (iii), **not** to a denial of real natural effects.\n2. **Crucial relation to Avicenna:** compare [*Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1–2](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_E_AVICENNA_VI1.md) and [VI.2](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md). Lāhījī adopts a structured Avicennian resolution of temporal novelties under an eternal source; he does not simply replace the natural world with instantaneous creation.\n3. **Link to form-bestowal:** [*Gawhar*, Chapter 6](LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md) explicitly assigns natural forms to the tenth intellect. Chapter 7 specifies how a new form appears at **this time**: material preparedness is completed and an existential gift arrives. The combined account is strikingly close in structure to Ibn Sīnā *Ilāhiyyāt* IX.5.\n4. **Historical limits:** the celestial mechanics belongs to premodern cosmology and is not empirically vindicated by translation; the metaphysical distinctions of temporal determination, existential dependence and causal priority can be analysed independently.\n5. **Source boundary:** this is a continuous, short Chapter 7 as printed on pp.304–306, translated with selected reported print variants; it is **not** an edition of the entire Persian work or an original manuscript collation.\n\n**Result:** Direct Persian evidence for a mature later Avicennian account of the **relation of temporal effects to eternal causes**, with a precise distinction between existential and temporal causal explanation.\n","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/304?lang=ar","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_02_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_TEMPORAL_CAUSATION.md","sourceCoverage":"Persian anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"lah-divine","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_03_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_DIVINE_AGENCY.md","author":"ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī","work":"Gawhar-i murād","title":"Divine approval, purpose, and intention","locus":"II.3.8 pp.307–309","lang":"fa","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"bounded complete chapter","source":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/307?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/307?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/308?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/309?lang=ar"],"topics":["divine-will","causation","cosmology"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 03 — Lāhījī, *Gawhar-i murād*, II.3.8: divine willing and agency by satisfaction\n\n**31 October 2026 · Persian primary source → working English translation**\n\n**Witness**: *Gawhar-i murād*, Article II, Part 3, **Chapter 8**, printed Persian pp.307–309 in the Nashr-i Sāya edition: [307](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/307?lang=ar), [308](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/308?lang=ar), [309](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/309?lang=ar). Printed notes list A/B/C variants; these are reports in the edition, not newly inspected manuscripts.\n\n## Heading and p.307 — agency without self-perfection\n\n> فاعليّت خداى تعالى به علم و اراده است ، نه به طبع\n\n**Heading:** “On what mode of agency the Necessary Existent exercises with respect to things.”\n\nAll scholars agree that God's agency operates through knowledge and will, not through unconscious nature. Philosophers and critical theologians agree, moreover, that divine knowledge and will, like God's other attributes, are identical with the divine essence, not additional things. And they agree that God acts for no aim, profit, perfection or preference returning to the divine essence—far exalted is God above this. They disagree, however, whether God can intend such things **not for himself but for benefiting another**. The philosophers maintain that although divine acts contain the fullest goodness and the greatest benefit to creatures, God transcends an intention aimed at those benefits. They argue that intention concerns something in view of which an agent is better off, so that by acting the agent acquires a perfection which the agent lacked before; such acquisition cannot apply to a true first cause.\n\n## p.308 — physician, carpenter, *riḍā* versus *qaṣd*\n\nNo genuine cause becomes more perfect through its effect, for the effect's perfection comes from the cause: how could it in turn perfect the cause? Intention can proceed from an agent acting by will that is not the effect's **true existential cause**. Instead such an agent uses intention to make matter ready for an effect to receive existence from its true cause. Thus a physician prepares the patient's body for the influx of health from the **Giver of Health and Life**, and a carpenter prepares wood for the influx of the form of a bed from the **Giver of Forms**. In short, the agency of beings that act through animal willing is, strictly considered, only the moving of matter. It is appropriate to ascribe *intention* to these agents, not to the true cause. In a genuine existential cause, willing resolves into *riḍā* (approval or satisfaction), not *qaṣd* (purposeful intending). Consequently the Necessary Existent is an agent through *riḍā*, not through *qaṣd*, and the same holds for the true intermediary causes, namely separate intellects and celestial souls.\n\nThe majority of theologians, by contrast, describe God as acting through intention. Lāhījī criticises the Ashʿarites for rejecting purposes and denying the necessity that divine actions embody good and benefits, while the Muʿtazilites affirm ends directed toward others and deny that benefiting another necessarily completes the agent.\n\n## p.309 — an attempted reconciliation\n\nThe more exact analysis suggests that the Muʿtazilī position on benefit can be reduced to what the philosophers mean when they say divine works include wise purposes and benefits. Whether these are **intended** or **approved** may then be a merely verbal difference: Muʿtazilites do not restrict the technical meaning of intending as the philosophers do, and the philosophers call divine intention *riḍā* under their terminology. This reconciliation is particularly plausible in the case of those Muʿtazilites who affirm some degree of causal necessitation: the will necessarily entails the act and the act does not exist until necessary. That need not conflict with the philosophical account of acts as consequences of the goodness of the Necessary Existent's perfect essence. In that restricted comparison there may be no substantive disagreement.\n\n**Print variants**: p.307 B `بر` for `در` and omits `در`; p.308 A has `حقيقة` for `حقيقيه`, B `او` for `معلول`, A omits `در`, A omits `را`; p.309 C `بالمعنى` for `معنى`, B/C give `بناء بر تخصيص اصطلاح كنند` in the terminology sentence, A `تخصيص` for `تحقيق`. The p.309 variants affect the phrasing of the attempted reconciliation and deserve comparison with the actual manuscripts.\n\n## Apparatus — what cannot be collapsed\n\n1. **Not unqualified determinism.** Lāhījī denies that God's agency is blind natural compulsion, while accepting a type of causal necessitation consonant with the divine will under the *riḍā* model. Whether that leaves genuine alternative possibilities for creation needs a separate modal demonstration.\n2. **Two uses of “will.”** *Qaṣd* is deliberative intention directed to an end improving the agent; *riḍā* is an approving, non-acquisitive relation to effects. Translation as the undifferentiated “volition” would erase the substantive terminology.\n3. **A crucial later-Avicennian continuity.** The physician and carpenter prepare material dispositions; the true cause confers actuality. Compare Ibn Sīnā [*Ilāhiyyāt* VI.2](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_B_AVICENNA_VI2.md) and [IX.5](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_D_AVICENNA_IX5.md).\n4. **School generalizations are Lāhījī's historical characterisations**, not a modern proof that every Ashʿarī or Muʿtazilī held the position exactly as stated. In particular the closing reconciliation concerns only certain Muʿtazilites.\n5. **Boundary:** the entire brief Chapter 8 spans pp.307–309 in this witness. This new working translation closely follows the full connected argument, but no direct manuscript collation or independent verification of every cited theologian has been attempted.\n\n**Result:** a non-Ṣadrian late Avicennian account of **divine and intermediate agency by approval** that preserves natural preparatory agents, separates them from existential causes, and proposes a conditional rapprochement with Muʿtazilī teleology.\n","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/17438/307?lang=ar","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_03_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_DIVINE_AGENCY.md","sourceCoverage":"Persian anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"lah-sarmaya","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_08_LAHIJI_SARMAYA_IMAN_DEPENDENCE.md","author":"ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī","work":"Sarmāya-i īmān","title":"Continuous existence and the builder objection","locus":"pp.37–38","lang":"fa","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"bounded excerpt","source":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/17348/37","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/17348/37","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/17348/38"],"topics":["conservation","causation","modality"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 08 — Lāhījī, *Sarmāya-i īmān*: preference, continual existence and the builder\n\n**31 October 2026 · independent Persian theological work → new English translation**\n\n**Source:** ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Lāhījī, *Sarmāya-i īmān dar uṣūl-i iʿtiqādāt*, Persian text, [p.37](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/17348/37) and [p.38](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/17348/38); [p.39](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/17348/39) concludes a separate theological argument. This is **an independent work** from *Gawhar-i murād* and *Shawāriq al-ilhām*, not another copy of the same wording. The translation is focused on the contiguous two-page argument on preference, causality and conservation; the page-38 excerpt closes mid-proof at the p.39 transition.\n\n## p.37 — *awlawiyya* cannot explain an actual contingent effect\n\n> الشيء ما لم يجب لم يوجد\n\n**Translation of the argument:** Suppose an existence is preferable to nonexistence, whether that preference derives from the possible being's essence or from another cause. The contingent thing still cannot exist until its cause renders its existence *necessary*. If, given the preference, nonexistence is no longer possible, the alleged preference is really necessity and should be named as such. If nonexistence remains possible, the actual occurrence of existence requires another determinant; otherwise an equally possible alternative occurs without sufficient explanation. Introducing a second, third, or infinitely many “preferences” does not help. Either the totality of these makes nonexistence impossible, in which case necessity has been reached, or nonexistence remains possible and the difficulty recurs. In the hypothetical-syllogistic formulation: if preference alone sufficed for occurrence, non-occurrence would be impossible; but non-occurrence is possible in the case of a contingent thing. Therefore preference alone does not suffice. **“A thing does not exist until it has become necessary.”**\n\n## p.38 — why need persists as long as the contingent thing exists\n\n> ممكن ... در بقاء وجود نيز محتاج باشد به علت\n\n**Translation:** Once it is understood that the ground of dependence is contingency (*imkān*), not origination (*ḥudūth*), and that contingency is inseparable from a possible quiddity even after the thing exists, it follows that the contingent being needs a cause both at the beginning and throughout its continuance. Indeed existence flows into it afresh, instant after instant, from the cause: **persistence is nothing but continued existence**. At every instant in which existence occurs it must proceed from its cause. Without the true principle's giving existence, the whole world would fall away into complete nonbeing. \n\n**The builder objection and reply:** Some who make temporal origination the ground of need imagine a completed house or pot should disappear as soon as builder or potter dies, if continuing existence depended on a cause. But craftspeople are not the true existential efficient causes of houses and pots: they cause movements which **prepare** materials to receive form and existence from the bestower of forms and being. The absence of a preparatory cause does not imply the disappearance of the already constituted effect. Moreover, denying need during continuance damages the argument for the Necessary Existent: an allegedly beginningless contingent could terminate an explanatory chain without any cause.\n\n## Apparatus and comparisons\n\n1. **Important conceptual subtlety:** Lāhījī's imagery that existence is continually renewed “instant after instant” should not automatically be construed as Ṣadrā's **substantial motion**, or as Ashʿarite **re-creation of every accident**. In context the point is existential dependence; the mode of substantial persistence is a further question.\n2. **Two works, one coherent doctrine:** this independent Persian argument agrees with [*Gawhar-i murād* Chapter 7](LATE_FASCICLE_02_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_TEMPORAL_CAUSATION.md) about contingency and continuance and [Chapter 6](LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md) about a distinct bestower of forms. A historical author may formulate the same principle differently in a short creed and a long philosophical treatise.\n3. **Comparison:** Avicenna [*Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_E_AVICENNA_VI1.md) already states the argument; Lāhījī provides a Persian pedagogical exposition, not its first invention. Aquinas [*ST* I q.104](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_F_AQUINAS_ST104.md) likewise differentiates a builder's *fieri* agency and an existential conserving source, though his divine freedom doctrine diverges.\n4. **Document state:** direct searchable printed text consulted; modern print transcription, no manuscript variants established; p.37–38 translated as a bounded passage, not a full edition of the creed.\n\n**Result:** independent Persian corroboration from Lāhījī that preserving a contingent being is a **continuous act of dependence**, not something once brought about by a physical builder.\n","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/17348/37","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_08_LAHIJI_SARMAYA_IMAN_DEPENDENCE.md","sourceCoverage":"Persian anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"taft-mental","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_06_TAFTAZANI_MAQASID_MENTAL_EXISTENCE.md","author":"Saʿd al-Dīn al-Taftāzānī","work":"Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid","title":"Mental existence: three objections and answers","locus":"pp.78–79","lang":"ar","family":"Post-Tajrīd / Indian","kind":"late","coverage":"objections and replies complete","source":"https://usul.ai/ar/t/sharh-maqasid/78","sourceUrls":["https://usul.ai/ar/t/sharh-maqasid/78","https://usul.ai/ar/t/sharh-maqasid/79"],"topics":["mental-existence","knowledge","universals"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 06 — al-Taftāzānī, *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, the three objections to mental existence\n\n**31 October 2026 · Arabic primary source → working English translation**\n\n**Direct source:** Saʿd al-Dīn al-Taftāzānī (d. 792/1389), *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid fī ʿilm al-kalām*, printed ed. 1401/1981, pp. **78–79** as transcribed at [Usul p.78](https://usul.ai/ar/t/sharh-maqasid/78) and [p.79](https://usul.ai/ar/t/sharh-maqasid/79). **Boundary:** a connected **argument dossier** from the discussion of proofs and counterarguments for *al-wujūd al-dhihnī*, not the entire work or full surrounding chapter. The entire three-objection-and-answer sequence of p.79 is translated below in distinct units. The long preparatory discussion on p.78 is selectively translated with explicitly bounded sentences.\n\n## I. Arguments for mental existence — p.78 (selected connected unit)\n\n> أن الكلي مفهوم وكل مفهوم ثابت ضرورة تميزه عند العقل\n\n**Translation:** “The universal is conceived, and everything conceived has some establishment, necessarily, by virtue of its being distinguishable for the intellect.” But a universal as universal cannot be a singular thing in extra-mental reality, where everything is individuated; hence the kind of establishment in question is mental. Likewise certain genuinely universal affirmative judgments must have intelligible subjects despite lacking any external instance, as in the claim “Every phoenix is an animal.” Their subject cannot be restricted to extant particular birds.\n\n**The opponent's objection:** Predication does not require an externally or mentally *existent* subject: in predicating we may only mean that whatever answers to a subject term answers to the predicate; recognition of the terms is not yet proof of an additional mode of existence. The supposed three proofs collapse into the fact that we conceive what lacks an external instance. If the defender simply assumes that conception occurs through a thing's form being present in the intellect, the disputed thesis has been smuggled into a premise.\n\n**Reply:** There must be a relation between knowing intellect and object known, whether cognition is conceived as an acquired form, a special relation, or a relational quality. Relation to absolute nothingness is impossible; therefore the object known must possess at least some determinate standing. Since universals, absent things and impossibilia do not exist externally, their intelligible determination must lie elsewhere.\n\n## II. The rival location hypotheses — pp.78–79 (selected)\n\nSome propose independently subsisting Platonic forms, an imaginal world, or forms imprinted in a separate Active Intellect. Taftāzānī answers that what is absolutely impossible cannot subsist externally as an individual form or be imprinted in a separate intellect as a self-standing impossible individual. What that intellect could possess is **the conception** of the impossible, itself bringing the explanation back to representation. Yet the exact inference from **objectivity of conception** to a *form inhering in this knower* is contested; al-Taftāzānī reports that even the *Mawāqif* formulation may be disputed at precisely this point.\n\n## III. The deniers' three objections — p.79\n\n> الأول لو كان تصور الشيء مستلزما لحصوله في العقل\n\n**First objection, translation:** If imagining a thing requires its presence in the intellect, then conceiving heat and cold would make the intellect hot and cold. That is impossible, since it would combine contraries and attribute to the intellect properties of bodies.\n\n**Second objection:** The heavens, with all their vastness, would have to be present in the intellect when conceived, and in imagination when imagined. This is evidently false.\n\n**Third objection:** Conceiving nonexistent things would make them exist externally, since they exist in an intellect that itself exists outside the mind, and what exists *inside* a thing existing outside the mind is supposed to exist where that thing is—just as water in a jug in a house is in that house.\n\n## IV. The answer — p.79\n\n> الفرق بين الوجود المتأصل ... والوجود ... الذهني\n\n**Translation:** All three objections fail to distinguish **rooted, extra-mental existence**—through which a thing has its concrete individuality—from the non-rooted existence of a **mental form**. The thing that is literally hot is a subject in which the extra-mental reality of heat subsists, not one that possesses its mental form; the opposition of heat and cold concerns their external realities, not their representations. What plainly cannot be contained within an intellect or imagination is the *actual cosmic reality* of the heavens, not the universal or particular form through which they are grasped. Finally, the inference from an interior to an enclosing exterior applies when both are independently existing concrete realities (water, jug, house), not when an absent object's **form** has mental being while the mind has external being. The mental representation and the external individual differ in their necessary concomitants: the former may be universal, abstract and causally inactive, the latter individuated and productive of effects. Their *correspondence* means that a quiddity actualised externally would be that external reality, and stripped of individuating attachments could be considered as the intelligible quiddity.\n\n## V. Editorial and philosophical apparatus\n\n1. **The direct voice is Taftāzānī.** A nested report of al-Ījī's *Mawāqif* is identified as such, and an objection to it remains Taftāzānī's presentation. We do not count it as independently collated al-Ījī text.\n2. **Key terms:** `wujūd aṣīl / mutaʾaṣṣil` is extra-mental, concrete actuality; `wujūd dhihnī` is mental representational actuality; `ṣūra` need not be a second physical individual; `hawiyya ʿayniyya` is extra-mental individuated reality. The source's `ʿāriḍ` argument should not be paraphrased as “ideas are physical heat.”\n3. **Cross-commentary test:** compare [Jurjānī's *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif*](LATE_FASCICLE_07_MAWAQIF_JURJANI_GLOSSES.md) and [Lāhījī's *Shawāriq* mental-existence argument](chapters/01-mental-existence-and-quiddity.md). The logical difference concerns what is **represented** versus the representing act's ontological inherence.\n4. **Real issue not settled automatically:** one can reject the opponents' crude physical objections and still debate whether the intelligible species is formally identical with the represented nature, whether conception entails mental *wujūd* or a merely intentional relation, and what constitutes *nafs al-amr* for true judgments about impossible objects.\n5. **Boundaries:** p.79 provides the direct sequence of three objections and the connected answer, translated without eliding a substantive objection. The surrounding discussion p.78 is selective; no manuscript witnesses or variant readings were checked.\n\n**Status:** new complete bounded objections-and-answer sequence from a primary post-Avicennian kalām commentary, not an exhaustive translation of *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*.\n","sourceUrl":"https://usul.ai/ar/t/sharh-maqasid/78","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_06_TAFTAZANI_MAQASID_MENTAL_EXISTENCE.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"jurj-mental","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_07_MAWAQIF_JURJANI_GLOSSES.md","author":"al-Ījī / al-Jurjānī / unnamed marginal layers","work":"al-Mawāqif with Sharḥ","title":"Mental being in the Mawāqif commentary","locus":"vol.VI pp.7–8","lang":"ar","family":"Post-Tajrīd / Indian","kind":"late","coverage":"layered excerpt attribution unresolved","source":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9299/7?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9299/7?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9299/8?lang=ar"],"topics":["mental-existence","knowledge","universals"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 07 — *al-Mawāqif* and al-Jurjānī's *Sharḥ*, with the printed Siyālkūtī–Ḥalabī marginal layers\n\n**31 October 2026 · Arabic primary text → working layered English translation**\n\n**Witness**: al-Ījī, *al-Mawāqif*, within al-Sayyid al-Sharīf al-Jurjānī's *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif*, printed **vol. VI**, pp. **7–8**, in a composite offset edition labelled “with the two marginal glosses of al-Siyālkūtī and al-Ḥalabī”; searchable [page 7](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9299/7?lang=ar), [page 8](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9299/8?lang=ar). **Editorial caution:** parenthesised lemmata are generally the *matn*, connected unparenthesised argument belongs to the commentator, and interleaved `قوله` notes belong to marginal layers—but this digital stream does **not** consistently identify which anonymous note belongs to which named glossator. Therefore we translate a clearly bounded exchange and do not claim any anonymous marginal sentence as directly verified Siyālkūtī's or Ḥalabī's.\n\n## p.7 — the deniers' arguments (matn and sharḥ)\n\n**Matn lemma (al-Ījī):**\n\n> وقال المتكلمون هو ... باطل لوجهين\n\n**Translation:** “The theologians held that this [equating cognition with mental existence] is false for two reasons.”\n\n**Al-Jurjānī's reported explanation:** “This” refers to the claim that knowledge consists in mental existence. First, if intellection consists in the essence or quiddity of the known thing being present in the knower's intellect, someone who understands blackness and whiteness and judges them contraries would possess them both mentally. The intellect would then itself be black and white, since being black or white is simply the possession of such qualities; it would moreover house two contraries in one subject, an absurdity. Second, the actual quiddities of a mountain and of the heavens plainly cannot occupy the narrow mind: to concede the presence of their vast actual magnitudes is to deny the evident.\n\n**Embedded gloss, attribution unresolved:** the editor's interleaved note argues that, considered simply as a quiddity, a nature can be abstracted from its specifically *mental* determination; when considered precisely *as mental*, it cannot acquire extra-mental existence under the same individuating mental identity. Another note asks whether the physical impossibility of a vast thing fitting in a little body really proves that a representation cannot occur; the answer depends on the precise sense of “occurring within”.\n\n## p.8 — the commentator's paired replies\n\n**First reply (matn lemma and Jurjānī's expansion):**\n\n> لو حصل فيه هوية السواد والبياض ... لا ماهيتهما\n\nThe objection would follow only if the concrete *external realities* of blackness and whiteness were present within the intellect, not their mentally existing intelligible quiddities. A mental form exists in a dependent, representational way; actual physical colour, physical opposition, and real inherence presuppose the extra-mental reality. Thus the mind does not literally become two coloured bodies or receive mutually excluding physical qualities when it understands colour.\n\n**Second reply:**\n\n> الممتنع حصول هوية الجبل والسماء\n\nWhat cannot be received by intellect or imagination is the enormous **external identity and magnitude** of mountain and heaven; it does not follow that their intelligible representations—universal or individual—are too large to be understood.\n\n**Marginal objections and replies, anonymous within the composite stream:** Some distinguish “occurring in the mind” as a container-like relation from a physical quality's inhering in a subject. A critic replies that the subject of knowledge must nonetheless be described as knowledgeable, so mere containment does not eliminate the issue. Others invoke a mirror containing a small image of an immense object; an answer distinguishes optical reflection from a bodily image literally stamped in a surface. Still other marginal notes insist that the solution ultimately depends on which effects belong **only to extra-mental existence** and do not belong to a mental representation.\n\n## Edition-critical controls\n\n1. **Three historical layers, one concatenated digital stream.** (a) al-Ījī's *matn*; (b) al-Jurjānī's *sharḥ*; (c) al-Siyālkūtī and al-Ḥalabī marginalia, together with possibly further interleaving. The host labels the *book* but does not identify every note. No independent source-witness division was verified visually; do not pretend the third layer has been separated by author.\n2. **The gender of “thing present”.** One can understand heat without becoming literally hot; a represented nature does not possess in the knower all the extra-mental accidents that its outward instance would produce. This is the same logical answer [Taftāzānī](LATE_FASCICLE_06_TAFTAZANI_MAQASID_MENTAL_EXISTENCE.md) gives to a similar objection, though the texts' exact dependence is not demonstrated merely by parallel content.\n3. **Interpretation of *nafs al-amr*.** The distinction between quiddity considered absolutely and quiddity qualified by mental or external existence is not yet a proof of an independently subsisting “third world”; it is a discipline of different respects (*iʿtibārāt*).\n4. **What to edit next:** inspect a physical scan of this composite edition and compare separately identified al-Siyālkūtī and al-Ḥalabī gloss prints, then align folio-level entries with the present p.7–8 digital concatenation. Recompute speaker labels at that time.\n5. **Extent:** two directly inspected printed pages, one complete objection–reply *topic*, **not** a diplomatic critical edition of all four distinct authors or of the complete sixth volume.\n\n**Status:** layered working translation and attribution audit. Real post-*Tajrīd* scholastic material incorporated without manufacturing anonymous Indian glosses.\n","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9299/7?lang=ar","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_07_MAWAQIF_JURJANI_GLOSSES.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"taha-jal","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_05_INDIAN_TAHANAWI_MIR_ZAHID_JAL.md","author":"Muḥammad ʿAlī al-Tahānawī (reports Mīr Zāhid)","work":"Kashshāf iṣṭilāḥāt al-funūn","title":"Indian debates on simple and compound making","locus":"vol.I pp.620–621","lang":"ar","family":"Post-Tajrīd / Indian","kind":"late","coverage":"primary entry with nested mir zahid report","source":"https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%B9%D8%A9-%D9%83%D8%B4%D8%A7%D9%81-%D8%A7%D8%B5%D8%B7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85/v/1/p/620","sourceUrls":["https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%B9%D8%A9-%D9%83%D8%B4%D8%A7%D9%81-%D8%A7%D8%B5%D8%B7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85/v/1/p/620","https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%B9%D8%A9-%D9%83%D8%B4%D8%A7%D9%81-%D8%A7%D8%B5%D8%B7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85/v/1/p/621"],"topics":["existence","causation","modality"],"attribution":"direct later witness with reported earlier voices","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 05 — Indian philosophical lexicography on *jaʿl*: al-Tahānawī and the reported Mīr Zāhid gloss\n\n**31 October 2026 · Arabic primary text → new working translation with attribution control**\n\n**Direct witness:** Muḥammad ʿAlī al-Tahānawī (Indian scholar, eighteenth century), *Kashshāf iṣṭilāḥāt al-funūn wa-l-ʿulūm*, vol. I, entry **الجعل**, opening on [printed p.620](https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%B9%D8%A9-%D9%83%D8%B4%D8%A7%D9%81-%D8%A7%D8%B5%D8%B7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85/v/1/p/620) and continuing/ending [p.621](https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%B9%D8%A9-%D9%83%D8%B4%D8%A7%D9%81-%D8%A7%D8%B5%D8%B7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85/v/1/p/621). **Reported embedded witness:** **Mīr Zāhid**, gloss on al-Jurjānī's *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif* in the section on general matters and the question “Is quiddity made?”. The two are **not independent direct collations**: *Kashshāf* is the direct witness here and Mīr Zāhid is cited *within it*.\n\n## Entry's first distinction — simple and compound making\n\n> وهو عند الحكماء على قسمين: جعل بسيط ... وجعل مركب\n\n**Translation of the connected definitions:** For the philosophers, “making” (*jaʿl*) is of two types. In **simple making** a thing itself is produced: its effect is the thing itself, requiring only one term, the thing made. Its upshot is bringing something from nonexistence into existence. In **compound making**, one makes a thing *be something*: the effect is what the copulative construction signifies, the quiddity's being qualified by existence, considered not as an independently conceived thing but as the relation through which the two terms are grasped. This requires both a thing made and a determination it is made to have.\n\n## The South Asian report of the classical debate\n\n> كذا ذكر ميرزا زاهد في حاشية شرح المواقف في الأمور العامة\n\n**Translation:** “So Mīrzā Zāhid stated in his gloss on *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif*, among the General Matters.”\n\nThe *Kashshāf* attributes to **Illuminationists** the first view: the agent makes the quiddity itself; and to **Peripatetics** the second: the agent makes a quiddity *existent*. **This is the position-labeling transmitted by Tahānawī on Mīr Zāhid's authority**, not an independent survey of every Illuminationist and Peripatetic.\n\n## The dispute and its arguments — p.621\n\n**For simple making**, three arguments are transmitted. First, one must arrive at simple making directed to existence or its attribution: whatever is supposed to be made is itself a quiddity, and treating every such quiddity as requiring a further act threatens regress. Second, existence and the relation of existence to the quiddity are conceptual considerations, whereas what the making effects seems to be real. Third, existence is predicated of the Necessary Existent through its own reality; of a contingent quiddity, only insofar as it is related to a producer. Were the possible quiddity independent of the producer in itself, existence would be predicable at the rank of its own essence, destroying its contingency.\n\n**For compound making**, two arguments are given. First, there cannot be a making that intervenes between a quiddity and itself; the text observes that this argument fails if one mistakenly construes *simple* making as though it were a third relation inserted between two already actual things. Second, the ground of causal need is **possibility**, understood by a relation of existence to quiddity; hence the quiddity is made in respect of its existence, not simply in respect of its being a quiddity.\n\n**Reply and further study:** The final answer observes that possibility explains dependence *with respect to existence* and does not thereby rule out dependence in other respects. The entry refers the reader to the glosses on *al-Sullam* and to the **Zāhidiyya glosses on *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif*** for fuller analysis. This final appeal demonstrates the functioning of an Indian *ḥāshiya* network rather than supplying the original Mīr Zāhid text in full.\n\n## Variant apparatus\n\n- The modern edition's note on p.621 has `أحقية` for the main `حقية` in the introduction to the simple-making proofs; another note has `يعرض` for `يفرض` in the first proof.\n- The printed editor identifies Mīr Zāhid's gloss on the second *Mawqif* (general matters). This is useful witness identification but the edition's biographical footnote is not the original author's wording.\n- Causal “simple” and “compound” *jaʿl* must not be conflated with **making a simple vs complex essence**. The former distinguishes the logical shape of making, the latter its object.\n\n## Comparison to the longer Iranian *Tajrīd* reception\n\nThe argument should be compared with the already translated [Lāhījī, *Shawāriq* I:158–161](chapters/02-making-and-causal-explanation.md). Both discuss whether the actuality of a contingent individual or its quiddity *as such* is produced; the Indian entry reports a Mīr Zāhid scholastic classification, while Lāhījī independently develops objections and replies in the *Tajrīd* commentary line. **Close similarity does not prove direct textual copying.**\n\n**Edition limits:** this is a new, bounded English working translation of the *jaʿl* entry's technical divisions and argument units with identified source pages. The original Mīr Zāhid *ḥāshiya* has **not** been collated independently; do not treat the nested testimony as a second directly inspected Persian/Indian manuscript. No source-manuscript autopsy is claimed.\n","sourceUrl":"https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%B9%D8%A9-%D9%83%D8%B4%D8%A7%D9%81-%D8%A7%D8%B5%D8%B7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85/v/1/p/620","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_05_INDIAN_TAHANAWI_MIR_ZAHID_JAL.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"astar-choice","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_09_POST_TAJRID_QUṢHJI_KHAFRI_KHWANSARI_POWER.md","author":"Muḥammad Jaʿfar al-Astarābādī (reports Ṭūsī, Qūshjī, al-Khafrī, al-Khwānsārī)","work":"al-Barāhīn al-qāṭiʿa","title":"Choice and necessity in the post-Tajrīd lineage","locus":"vol.II pp.96–99","lang":"ar","family":"Post-Tajrīd / Indian","kind":"late","coverage":"primary commentary with nested reports","source":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=718&page=91&type=c_fbook","sourceUrls":["https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=718&page=91&type=c_fbook"],"topics":["divine-will","modality","causation"],"attribution":"direct later witness with reported earlier voices","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 09 — Power, necessity and choosing: al-Ṭūsī → Qūshjī → al-Khafrī → Khwānsārī in a later *Tajrīd* commentary\n\n**31 October 2026 · Arabic scholastic argument-chain translated with separately named layers**\n\n**Direct witness:** Muḥammad Jaʿfar al-Astarābādī, *al-Barāhīn al-qāṭiʿa*, vol. II, printed pp. **96–99**, online [large transcription whose page marker 96 introduces the argument](https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=718&page=91&type=c_fbook). **This is the author directly read in this fascicle.** The text itself **reports or quotes** the distinct historical positions of the *Tajrīd* author Naṣīr al-Dīn al-Ṭūsī, al-Ḥillī's *Kashf al-murād*, ʿAlī Qūshjī's *Sharḥ al-Tajrīd* (cited there pp.310–311), Ghiyāth al-Dīn al-Khafrī (manuscript fol.5), and Āqā Jamāl al-Khwānsārī (via another gloss). Until those original publications/manuscripts are individually verified, the present document is **one directly read later author with a nested transmission chain**, not five independent source collations.\n\n## 1. The challenge: if the cause is complete, can it choose? (p.96–97)\n\n**Objection, in the direct text (working translation):** Divine power is understood as the possibility of both acting and refraining. But an effect of an agent must either be necessary or impossible: when the agent possesses every condition for effectiveness and is a complete cause, its effect necessarily proceeds, since a complete cause cannot exist without its effect. Without these conditions the effect is impossible. Thus there appears to be no interval in which doing and refraining are both possible, and divine power seems impossible.\n\n**Ṭūsī's lemma, as embedded by Astarābādī:**\n\n> ويمكن عروض الوجوب والإمكان للأثر بالاعتبارين\n\n**Translation:** “Necessity and possibility may both apply to the effect under two different considerations.”\n\n**Qūshjī's explanation, as *reported* by the later author:** The effect, considering the power and the agent's essence without the determining will, is **possible**; considering the will directed to the most fitting action, it becomes **necessary**. This is *necessity through choosing*, and such necessity does not negate choice but expresses its efficacy. A capable agent is one who, on willing to act, produces the act; on willing not to act, refrains. The effect is also impossible under the consideration that its conditions are absent. The three modal predicates do not contradict each other because the respect differs. The later text cites both Qūshjī and al-Ḥillī's *Kashf al-murād* for substantially this response, not an identical verbatim passage in each.\n\n## 2. Al-Khafrī's objection to the reconciliation (p.97–98)\n\n**Embedded objection, expressly attributed to al-Khafrī:** If divine will is *identical to the divine essence*, it cannot fail to be present. Then the necessity arising from such will is incompatible with “power” understood in the theologians' stronger sense of genuinely being able to do or leave undone. “Necessity through will” may coexist with the philosophers' **conditional** definition of choice (“if he wills he acts, and if he does not will he refrains”), but since the eternal divine will does in fact will, the effect proceeds eternally from the willing essence. A complete cause cannot be detached from its effect in time; how, then, could a will identical with the divine essence be separated temporally from its willing a created effect? The text demands a real solution rather than a merely verbal appeal to choice.\n\n**Witness boundary:** the Astarābādī note cites al-Khafrī's *Ḥāshiya ʿalā Ilāhiyyāt Sharḥ al-Qūshjī*, folio 5, **as a manuscript lead**, not a newly inspected manuscript.\n\n## 3. The later author's response and the Khwānsārī pressure (p.98)\n\n**Astarābādī's own response:** The operative will producing an effect is an additional, newly arising *mashīʾa*, rather than precisely the essential attribute of divine knowledge. Consequently one may reject al-Khafrī's alleged incompatibility. Another solution emphasises the object's *being the best thing to create at a specified time*: the effect willed is a temporal thing, not an eternal thing, so its lack of eternity need not imply that the cause was incomplete. Further arguments refer to limits in the receptive object rather than limits in the agent.\n\nThe author then reports an objection associated through his notes with **Āqā Jamāl al-Khwānsārī**: if a possible thing's eternal existence is intrinsically impossible, why would its essence require absence only at one time rather than forever? Positing time-specific absence threatens to presuppose time itself. Here the **source layer is Astarābādī's report of a prior marginal controversy**. It is not yet textual evidence that Āqā Ḥusayn al-Khwānsārī (Jamāl's father) authored this objection; the two must never be conflated.\n\n## 4. Further reply and rival reading (pp.98–99)\n\nA further scholastic gloss (attributed in the text only by a title, “Sun of the Marginalists”) reformulates the problem from the **effect's own contingency**. Whether the cause's conditions are present or absent, the effect remains contingent *in itself*; it becomes necessary through a completed cause and impossible through the absence of required conditions. There is no contradiction, because intrinsic possibility excludes intrinsic necessity and intrinsic impossibility, but not **necessity or impossibility through another**. The gloss criticizes Qūshjī for framing the problem too strongly in terms of an effect's possible **proceeding** rather than the effect's intrinsic modal status.\n\n## Attribution and critical status\n\n| Layer | Historical locus named by the direct witness | Editorial strength |\n|---|---|---|\n| Matn | Ṭūsī, *Tajrīd* lemma `wa-yumkin ʿurūḍ al-wujūb wa-l-imkān ...` | Phrase directly present in the later witness; original *Tajrīd* to collate |\n| Reported sharḥ | Qūshjī, *Sharḥ al-Tajrīd* 310–311; Ḥillī, *Kashf al-murād* from p.280 | Reported exposition, not independent direct editions |\n| Reported supergloss | Khafrī, *Ḥāshiya* on Qūshjī, fol.5 | Reported manuscript quotation; locate folio/witness |\n| Reported Khwānsārī layer | Āqā **Jamāl** al-Khwānsārī, cited from prior gloss discussion p.159 | Mediated objection; not Āqā **Ḥusayn** |\n| Direct author | Muḥammad Jaʿfar al-Astarābādī, *al-Barāhīn al-qāṭiʿa*, II:96–99 | Printed text directly accessed; main current translation witness |\n\n**Synthesis:** this is concrete evidence that post-*Tajrīd* commentators did not merely repeat a formula “a possible effect becomes necessary through a complete cause.” They contested **which modal respect** is relevant to genuine freedom, whether divine will is identical with divine essence, whether a temporal effect is a different intentional object from an eternal one, and whether one can satisfy a theological requirement of alternate possibilities by a conditional definition of power.\n\n**Next collation obligations:** Qūshjī *Sharḥ al-Tajrīd* pp.310–311, al-Ḥillī *Kashf* p.280ff, Khafrī fol.5, the Astarābādī-cited Khwānsārī supergloss and its editor, then compare to [Lāhījī's *Gawhar* on divine agency](LATE_FASCICLE_03_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_DIVINE_AGENCY.md). Do not call these five separate primary-witness translations until their own Arabic texts are read.\n\n**Status:** **one directly verified later Arabic source, multi-author quotations/reports translated and indexed with provenance**, no independent manuscript collation.\n","sourceUrl":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=718&page=91&type=c_fbook","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_09_POST_TAJRID_QUṢHJI_KHAFRI_KHWANSARI_POWER.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"nara-actual","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_10_NARAQI_LAMAAT_EXISTENCE_ESSENCE.md","author":"Muḥammad Mahdī al-Narāqī","work":"al-Lamaʿāt al-ʿarshiyya","title":"Existence, quiddity and actuality","locus":"pp.72 and 82, noncontiguous","lang":"ar","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"two bounded selected arguments","source":"https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%A9/v/1/p/72","sourceUrls":["https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%A9/v/1/p/72","https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%A9/v/1/p/82"],"topics":["existence","quiddity","unity"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 10 — Narāqī, *al-Lamaʿāt al-ʿarshiyya*: existence, quiddity and the priority of actuality\n\n**31 October 2026 · Arabic primary source → new English translations**\n\n**Direct witness:** Muḥammad Mahdī al-Narāqī (d. 1795), *al-Lamaʿāt al-ʿarshiyya*, publicly readable scholarly edition. Two **distinct bounded argument units**, printed [p.72: objection to primacy of existence](https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%A9/v/1/p/72) and [p.82: how quiddity unites with existence](https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%A9/v/1/p/82). They are **not adjacent continuous pages**; this is a paired selection, not a complete chapter. The online edition's contents distinguishes origination of quiddities, possibility within particular existence, and unity as separate topics; do not treat them as one continuous lemma.\n\n## I. An objection to the primacy of existence and Narāqī's reply (p.72)\n\n> اتصاف الماهية بالوجود أمر عقلي\n\n**Translation of the objection:** Suppose existence is said to be primary and quiddity secondary. How does existence attach to a quiddity? If it precedes the quiddity, a predicate would exist before its subject; if it follows the quiddity, the quiddity would already exist and existence would be superfluous or require a further existence, causing regress; if existence and quiddity coexist independently, quiddity would not be existent *through* it, and another existence would again be needed.\n\n**Reply:** The quiddity's being qualified by existence is a **consideration of the intellect**, not a physical attribution like blackness attaching to a body, as though quiddity and existence were two distinct external things with separate subsistence. They are externally one: existence is actual through itself and quiddity exists through its identity with that actuality. The agent produces the **particular existence of the thing**, which is itself the referent to which the quiddity is truly predicated. Thus neither temporal precedence, temporal posteriority nor a separate simultaneity applies between two external entities. The meaning of “existence is prior to quiddity” is that it is fundamental in actuality and production, and is the reality grounding predication of essential notions. This priority is analogous to what is primarily vs derivatively true, not cause preceding effect or receptor preceding received thing.\n\n**Further implication:** particular contingent existences contain real respects of limitation and deficiency relative to absolute true being; from these real limits external quiddities can be abstracted. Quiddities therefore are not merely arbitrary fictions of the mind, but their intelligible form expresses the way contingent actuality is limited.\n\n## II. Contingent existence and the possibility of abstraction (p.82)\n\n> فكل وجود ممكن متضمن جهة عدمية يقتضيها بمحض حقيقته\n\n**Translation:** Every contingent particular existence includes a privative or limiting respect that belongs to it precisely as the kind of finite existence it is. The intellect abstracts a quiddity from that limiting respect. Without such an element no finite quiddity could be abstracted, as in the case of the Necessary Existent. Hence every existence from which a quiddity can be abstracted has composition or differentiation in this sense. Once we accept that each possible being's existence and quiddity are **identical in concrete reality**, we must nevertheless deny that their *concepts* are identical. Otherwise “existence” and “human being” would be synonymous, predicating existence of humanity would teach us nothing, and one could not think of one while abstracting from the other—all plainly false. Therefore existence and quiddity are conceptually distinct, while externally identical as the one individuated reality.\n\nThe remaining problem, Narāqī remarks, is to explain the way quiddity is attributed to existence **within intellectual analysis** without reintroducing a false external relation between distinct things.\n\n## Critical commentary\n\n1. **Not a simple contrast “Avicenna teaches essences, Ṣadrā teaches existences”.** Here a later non-monolithic Avicennian scholar adopts terminology characteristic of the *primacy of existence* and specifies the external unity of existence and quiddity. Do not assume he therefore accepts every further Sadrian thesis.\n2. **What is made (*majʿūl*)?** At p.72 the agent directly produces the **particular existence**, not an independently real generic concept of being. Compare [Lāhījī's *Shawāriq* debate](chapters/02-making-and-causal-explanation.md) and the [Indian Tahānawī/Mīr Zāhid gloss](LATE_FASCICLE_05_INDIAN_TAHANAWI_MIR_ZAHID_JAL.md).\n3. **What is real in limitation?** Narāqī does not claim an externally separable entity “nonbeing” is added to a piece of existence. The limit is an actual grounding respect (*ḥaythiyya wāqiʿiyya*) by which quiddity is intelligibly abstracted. This qualifies both crude nominalism and reification of quiddities.\n4. **Relation to Aquinas:** the real/nonreal distinction and priority of actuality may be compared with Aquinas' *actus essendi*, but Narāqī's external identity of particular existence with quiddity is **not automatically** Aquinas' account of a real composition of essence and esse. The analogy requires metaphysical argument.\n5. **Source control:** two directly inspected edited Arabic pages, with short original Arabic anchors and new English translations. They are discrete original arguments; no claim of contiguous edition of the intervening pages or manuscript-level variants.\n\n**Result:** primary evidence for selective appropriation of existence-first metaphysics in a late Persian scholar, going materially beyond the old shorthand “all later Avicennism remained quiddity-first.”\n","sourceUrl":"https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%A9/v/1/p/72","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_10_NARAQI_LAMAAT_EXISTENCE_ESSENCE.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"nara-second","file":"LATE_FASCICLE_11_NARAQI_SHARH_SHIFA_SECOND_INTENTIONS.md","author":"Muḥammad Mahdī al-Narāqī","work":"Sharḥ Ilāhiyyāt al-Shifāʾ","title":"Second intentions and the subject of metaphysics","locus":"vol.I pp.106–107 plus p.110 variant report","lang":"ar","family":"Late Persian","kind":"late","coverage":"bounded argument and discrete variant","source":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/15109/138?lang=ar","sourceUrls":["https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/15109/138?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/15109/139?lang=ar","https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/15109/142?lang=ar"],"topics":["mental-existence","universals","knowledge"],"attribution":"direct edited-text witness","status":"working translation; no independent manuscript collation","md":"# Late-commentator fascicle 11 — Narāqī's *Sharḥ Ilāhiyyāt al-Shifāʾ*: second intentions, being and manuscript-level negation\n\n**31 October 2026 · Arabic primary commentary → new English translation**\n\n**Direct witness:** Muḥammad Mahdī al-Narāqī, *Sharḥ al-Ilāhiyyāt min Kitāb al-Shifāʾ*, vol. I, edition of Ḥāmid Nājī Iṣfahānī, Qum, Congress of the Narāqī Scholars, 1380 Solar Hijrī; [printed p.106](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/15109/138?lang=ar), [p.107](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/15109/139?lang=ar), [a separate textual note at p.110](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/15109/142?lang=ar). The **connected argument unit** is pp.106–107; the textual note at p.110 is a separate later anchor, not hidden inside the translated continuous sequence. The online witness is a modern edited text; no fresh manuscript images collated.\n\n## A. What is a common metaphysical attribute? p.106\n\n> والإعتباريات ليس لها وجود في الخارج وإنّما وجودها وجود ماينتزع عنه\n\n**Reported distinction and Narāqī's analysis:** Some distinguish general metaphysical attributes from other accidents as follows. The first are actual beings, whether substances or accidents, bodily or mental. The latter are descriptions whose existence is **identical with the existence of their subjects**, even though the mind distinguishes and defines them by abstracting them from existing things. They are *iʿtibārī* and *intizāʿī*, things of consideration and abstraction: they have no extra reality detached from what they describe. By contrast a real accident has its existence within a subject but also differs from the subject's identity. This must be qualified, Narāqī says, because the division will not work straightforwardly for unity and plurality as understood by Ibn Sīnā, nor for all second intentions, some of which are indeed conceptual abstractions. The distinction needs a stricter criterion.\n\n**Objection:** Is the proposed criterion merely a way of naming the categories without clarifying the difference in their realities?\n\n**Answer:** A general metaphysical attribute belongs to an existent **as existent**, without requiring it to be prepared for a special kind of further accident. A second intention, however, is intelligible **only as predicated of a prior intelligible object** (*maʿqūl*), not simply of being as such.\n\n## B. Why metaphysics is the home of this inquiry, p.107\n\nNarāqī continues: a second intention cannot be understood except as an attribute of another concept, whether or not that attribute is also maximally general. Accordingly general metaphysical attributes and second intentions overlap, but neither is simply identical with the other in every respect. He then explains an Avicennian statement that some things must be **defined and established in the soul**: either their boundaries and intellectual truth must be made known by conception and assent, or a well-defined meaning must attain determinacy in thought through investigation. Such notions are not self-evident; they occur as common principles in many sciences, yet none of the particular sciences has them as an exclusive subject or proper attributes. Their definition and truth therefore belong to the science with the most general subject, **being qua being**.\n\n**Textual note**: the p.107 main edition marks a variant `تصديقنا` in witness F against `تصديقاً`; its logical impact is slight, though it changes the grammatical construction of conceptual knowledge and assent.\n\n## C. A genuine printed witness variant on negation, p.110 (separate supplementary locus)\n\n> ثمّ في بعض النّسخ وجدت لفظة « لا » قبل « يكون »\n\nNarāqī reports directly that **some copies contain the word “not” before “is/exists.”** He takes it likely to be a scribal slip but explains how the sentence can be understood if the variant is retained: the distinction would then concern inherent versus extrinsic properties of substances, not the same comparison under the first reading. This is a textual-critical report **by the historical commentator**, not a newly discovered manuscript variant of our own. The attribution should remain exactly this strong: *Narāqī reports* that copies differ and proposes a possible reconciliation.\n\n## Apparatus and comparison\n\n- **Ontological subtlety:** *iʿtibārī* need not mean “false” or “unfounded”; an abstract property may be grounded in the reality of its subject without constituting an additional independently existing thing.\n- **Why this matters for the project:** later Avicennian metaphysics inherits Avicenna's distinction between being as such and the objects of special sciences, while refining the relation of **second intentions**, real accidents and general attributes. The distinction is central to *nafs al-amr*, metaphysical predication, and ontology of relation.\n- **Two kinds of “in mind”:** a concept's being an object of second-order attribution is not identical to the act of imagining it; conflation would render the distinction between conceptual and external accidents uninformative.\n- **Attribution discipline:** Narāqī reports a prior account using `وقيل` (“it was said”), then criticises and qualifies it. Do not assign the whole reported definition to Narāqī without qualification.\n- **Scope:** pp.106–107 are translated as a connected bounded argument unit; p.110 supplies a separate verifiable textual-critical note. No independent manuscript collation. The historical work as published remains unfinished.\n\n**Result:** an original Narāqī commentary witness sharpening the metaphysical/logical distinction between what belongs to **being qua being** and what belongs to intelligibles considered as such.\n","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/15109/138?lang=ar","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/LATE_FASCICLE_11_NARAQI_SHARH_SHIFA_SECOND_INTENTIONS.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false},{"id":"khay-fire","file":"INDIAN_PRIMARY_QUOTATION_01_KHAYRABADI_HEAT.md","author":"Faḍl-i Ḥaqq al-Khayrābādī (through modern quotation)","work":"al-Hadiyya al-saʿīdiyya","title":"Five objections to latent fire","locus":"Cairo 1904 pp.92–94 as quoted in 2023","lang":"ar","family":"Indian","kind":"mediated","coverage":"mediated primary quotation; original uncollated","source":"https://downloads.hindawi.org/books/81461536.pdf#page=202","topics":["natural-generation","motion","matter"],"status":"do not count as independent 1904 witness","md":"# Indian documentary annex 01 — Faḍl-i Ḥaqq al-Khayrābādī on latent fire and material transformation\n\n**31 October 2026 · Mediated quotation of primary Arabic, with new English rendering**\n\n**Original author**: Muḥammad Faḍl al-Ḥaqq al-Khayrābādī (d. 1861/62), *al-Hadiyya al-saʿīdiyya fī al-ḥikma al-ṭabīʿiyya*, Cairo edition 1904, **pp.92–94**, as explicitly cited in Sāʾir Baṣma-jī, *Tārīkh ʿilm al-ḥarāra* (Hindawi 2023), **printed pp.202–203**. The modern book **quotes substantial Arabic argumentation** from the original; see [public modern secondary edition](https://downloads.hindawi.org/books/81461536.pdf#page=202), printed [p.202](https://downloads.hindawi.org/books/81461536.pdf#page=202) and [p.203](https://downloads.hindawi.org/books/81461536.pdf#page=203). The original 1904 work is catalogued at [UAE Digital Knowledge Center](https://ddl.ae/book/95961) but the historic printed pages were **not** directly accessed for collation. This annex is therefore **mediated primary testimony**, not a new direct 1904 witness or full edition. No modern book scans are redistributed.\n\n## The issue\n\nThe doctrine of **latent fire** (*kumūn al-nār*) seeks to explain heating as the entry or manifestation of previously concealed fiery substance. Khayrābādī challenges it by arguing that heating can arise through the **transformation** (*istiḥāla*) of an already present material constitution, not merely by ingress of invisible preexisting fire. Five phenomena are offered; the argument is a piece of nineteenth-century Indian scholastic **natural philosophy**, not yet evidence of his full position on the Active Intellect.\n\n### Five transmitted objections — new English rendering\n\n1. **Heat generated by violent motion.** Dry solids become hot when rubbed without a detectable foreign fire entering; analogous changes occur in rarefied air through forced compression/expansion and in violently agitated liquids. If every rise of temperature required imported fiery substance, such cases would be difficult to explain.\n2. **The permeability test.** When comparable liquids are heated in two vessels, one dense and the other porous, the latent-fire theory predicts that the porous vessel should admit the fiery influence faster. The observed outcome, he argues, does not follow that prediction.\n3. **The sealed vessel test.** A carefully closed vessel can become intensely hot although it lacks the entrance and exit paths supposedly required for a substantial influx of fire replacing other contents.\n4. **The bursting vessel.** When a tightly sealed flask containing water is heated, pressure develops until it may burst. The production of heat and pressure within the enclosure, without a commensurate exchange of imported fire and departing water, indicates internal transformation and generation. Khayrābādī notes that this argument and the preceding one overlap but are not logically identical: one establishes transformation, the other transformation **together with new generation**.\n5. **Ice cooling the region above it.** The colder parts do not naturally rise upward on their own, and no extraneous forcing agent is present; the cooling is better described as a transformation in the affected medium rather than a migration of a latent cooling substance.\n\n**Primary Arabic anchor phrases reproduced very briefly:** `الحركة العنيفة` (“violent movement”); `الاستحالة والكون معا` (“transformation and generation together”); `فإنه هو الاستحالة` (“for it is transformation”). The complete nineteenth-century passage was quoted in the linked modern study; this annex does not republish that modern book's extended quotation.\n\n## Critical analysis: what is demonstrated and what is not\n\n- **A meaningful Indian scholastic transformation debate exists.** The five arguments are an extended attempted refutation of an occult-substance interpretation of heating. They are empirically styled counterexamples within an Aristotelian–Avicennian taxonomy of forms, elemental natures, motion and qualitative alteration.\n- **Do not overinterpret their success.** Modern thermal physics cannot be inferred from this argument; frictional heating and phase change require mechanisms different from those Khayrābādī's inherited Aristotelian framework offers. The argument establishes the structure of his position, not its scientific truth.\n- **Disambiguate two changes.** Heating may be an *accidental qualitative alteration*; his reference to *kawn* in the bursting vessel goes further toward substantial generation. Neither is yet evidence that he rejects a separate intellect as the ontological giver of species forms. Such a conclusion requires the original natural-philosophical chapters on substantial forms.\n- **Manuscript and printing discrepancy:** modern catalogues give Cairo 1904 as the printing; the cited 2023 book refers to 1322 AH. Neither printing was directly consulted here. The original work's composition is often placed in the 1840s and must be distinguished from publication.\n- **Source-layer honesty:** the direct readable witness is **a 2023 modern quotation of a historical Arabic primary text**, with its original page references. The 1904 Arabic original remains uncollated; this is not one of the eleven directly translated Stage05 fascicles.\n- **Relation to prior research:** [Late commentators](late-commentators.html) · [Lāhījī on natural form-bestowal](LATE_FASCICLE_01_LAHIJI_GAWHAR_ACTIVE_INTELLECT.md) · [Ibn Sīnā on substantial material readiness](PRIMARY_TRANSLATION_FASCICLE_D_AVICENNA_IX5.md) · [Stage05 witness ledger](LATE_WITNESS_REGISTER_2026_10_31.json).\n\n**Status:** recovered and freshly rendered a five-part argument from a **secondary scholar's direct Arabic historical quotation**; original Khayrābādī source not independently inspected. This annex narrows the Indian evidence gap but does not close it.\n","sourceUrl":"https://downloads.hindawi.org/books/81461536.pdf#page=202","sourceUrls":["https://downloads.hindawi.org/books/81461536.pdf#page=202"],"sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/INDIAN_PRIMARY_QUOTATION_01_KHAYRABADI_HEAT.md","sourceCoverage":"Arabic anchors only","manuscriptCollated":false}],"unresolved":[{"id":"MI-1856","author":"Mīr Zāhid al-Harawī","work":"Ḥāshiya ʿalā Sharḥ al-Mawāqif — al-umūr al-ʿāmma","edition":"printed 1856, 106-page scan","status":"public-domain-scan-located-not-collated","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hashiya_Ala_Sharah_Al_Muwafiq_Umoor_E_Aamma_(IA_in.ernet.dli.2015.324001).pdf","evidence":"nested-report-in-Tahanawi-only"},{"id":"MI-1874","author":"Mīr Zāhid","work":"Ḥāshiya ʿalā Sharḥ al-Mawāqif","edition":"Delhi 1874, 79 pages","status":"catalogued-limited-access","url":"https://kutuphane.osmanlica.com/kitap/5250986"},{"id":"MI-1876","author":"Mīr Zāhid","work":"al-Ḥāshiya ʿalā Sharḥ al-Mawāqif","edition":"Lucknow 1876, 139 pages, editor Muḥammad Ṣādiq ʿAlī Lakhnawī","status":"catalogued-limited-access","url":"https://search.worldcat.org/title/alhashyh-lmyr-zahd-ala-shrh-almwaqf/oclc/978479874"},{"id":"KM-1879","author":"Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Ḥaqq al-Khayrābādī","work":"Sharḥ al-ḥāshiya al-Zāhidiyya ʿalā al-umūr al-ʿāmma","edition":"Kanpur c.1879–1880","status":"digitized-catalogued-not-transcribed","url":"https://dar.bibalex.org/en/BookDetail/BookDetail.aspx?keyword=DAF-Job%3A166828&queryParam=id"},{"id":"KH-1904","author":"Faḍl-i Ḥaqq al-Khayrābādī","work":"al-Hadiyya al-saʿīdiyya fī l-ḥikma al-ṭabīʿiyya","edition":"Cairo 1904 edited by ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Barqūqī","status":"digitized-scan-located-not-transcribed","url":"https://ddl.ae/book/95961","warning":"Do not infer separate form-bestowal position from secondary discussion of motion"},{"id":"KHW-1","author":"Āqā Ḥusayn al-Khwānsārī","work":"Ḥāshiya ʿalā Ilāhiyyāt al-Shifāʾ","status":"partial-edition-lead-not-collated","url":"https://almahdi.edu/wahid-amin/"},{"id":"KHW-2","author":"Āqā Jamāl al-Khwānsārī","work":"Gloss reported in al-Barāhīn al-qāṭiʿa","status":"nested-objection-only-not-independent-source","url":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=718&page=91&type=c_fbook","warning":"NOT Āqā Ḥusayn; distinguish father from son"},{"id":"JIL","author":"Mīrzā Abū al-Ḥasan Jilwa","work":"Ḥawāshī al-Shifāʾ / Ithbāt al-ḥaraka al-jawhariyya","status":"bibliography-lead-no-secure-passage","url":"https://seop.illc.uva.nl/archives/sum2016/entries/mulla-sadra/","warning":"Do not assert straightforward rejection of substantial motion without actual treatise"},{"id":"QSH","author":"ʿAlī Qūshjī","work":"Sharḥ al-Tajrīd","status":"nested-quotation-in-Astarabadi-original-witness-unverified","url":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=718&page=91&type=c_fbook","locator":"reported pp.310–311"},{"id":"KHAFRI","author":"Ghiyāth al-Dīn al-Khafrī","work":"Ḥāshiya ʿalā Ilāhiyyāt Sharḥ al-Qūshjī","status":"nested-quotation-manuscript-citation-not-verified","url":"https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=718&page=91&type=c_fbook","locator":"reported manuscript fol.5"},{"id":"QMB","author":"Qāḍī Mubārak Shāh","work":"Ḥāshiya ʿalā Ḥāshiyat Mīr Zāhid","status":"catalogued-manuscript-not-transcribed","url":"https://islamarchive.cc/manuscript/792038"}],"sourceWorksCount":8};
